WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.375
SWARAT CHAUDHURI:
In this course.

00:00:01.375 --> 00:00:06.330
You have seen a lot of
different kinds of uses of LLMs.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:11.870
And today, I'm going to
emphasize the use of LLMs

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:15.920
as a tool of scientific
and mathematical discovery.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.080
And in particular,
there's going to be

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.380
an emphasis on the
ability of LLMs

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:26.030
to discover abstract concepts
and how this can accelerate

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:28.880
these discovery processes.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.860
OK, so let us dive in.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:36.960
Let us start with the problem
of mathematical discovery.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.460
This is, of course, one
of the grandest challenges

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.520
known to humanity.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.170
Now, how does the process
of mathematical discovery

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:49.550
look like?

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:53.360
There is the-- first,
the process of modeling

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.810
where you are, getting
inspired by some sort

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:59.030
of a real-world
phenomenon, and then

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:03.460
modeling it in terms of
a variety of definitions

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.400
and concepts.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.010
Now, once you have a set of
definitions, at that point,

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:14.950
you may want to conjecture
various properties

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.340
of these definitions.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:22.220
And then these are potentially
candidate theorems and lemmas.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.500
And then there is this
process of rigorous reasoning

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.840
where you are actually
proving these statements.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.920
And this process is, for
human mathematicians,

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.420
intrinsically social.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:39.040
You are convincing each
other about the rigor

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.170
of your reasoning and the
value of your conjectures.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:45.070
But this process just
continues in this sort

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:46.870
of an iterative loop.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:50.750
So you may see proofs
and counterexamples,

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.200
and these may influence
your models as well as

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.140
your conjectures.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.125
And this process
just goes on, right?

00:01:58.125 --> 00:02:01.610
That the history of mathematics.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:04.970
Now, the dream of the
field of AI for math

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:08.840
is that you want to
automate substantial pieces

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.760
of this process,
and as a result,

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.770
you want to accelerate
this process even more.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.890
You want to be able to
discover new kinds of math.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:24.120
And this includes
settling known conjectures

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.940
but also potentially coming
up with new conjectures.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:33.510
And then there is the
setting of natural sciences,

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.720
that is also going
to be something

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.280
I'll cover in this lecture.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.660
And here, unlike
in math, you are

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:44.390
engaging with the real world.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.670
There is this process
of theory building

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:51.330
where you're coming up with
hypotheses about the universe,

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:56.320
and then you are
using these hypotheses

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.380
to design experiments, and
then you are experimentally

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:00.530
validating them, right?

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:05.750
And this experimental validation
process creates new data.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:10.400
And then this data leads
to new kinds of theories,

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.850
for example, by refinement
of the existing hypothesis.

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:16.000
Or in some cases, you
may want to just come up

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.010
with entirely new concepts
and new predictions.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:25.600
So the ambition of the
area of AI for science

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.380
is to automate hypothesis
generation and experiment

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:31.960
design.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:38.050
Now, the key ideas in
the areas of AI for math

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:42.760
and AI for science at this
point are that, number 1, search

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.630
is a really powerful concept.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:49.720
And so, we want to imagine
these spaces of hypothesis,

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:54.740
conjectures, and proofs and
systematically search them.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.590
Now, this is, of course,
a very massive space.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:03.590
There are all these
potential statements

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:05.340
about various
mathematical objects,

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.280
and there are all
these hypotheses that

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:08.790
are possible in the universe.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:12.150
And this space is
clearly infinite.

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:17.430
Now, how do you explore this
space in a tractable way?

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:21.300
Well, you use prior knowledge to
prioritize directions of search.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.760
And that's also
how humans proceed.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.520
We don't just do
discovery in a vacuum.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:32.480
We read existing papers, and
sometimes we incrementally

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:33.300
advance on them.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.770
And sometimes we
make a big leap.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:39.770
But that prior knowledge
is always of value.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:42.230
But then also we
learn from experience

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:46.730
how and how not to do
this sort of exploration.

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:51.980
You find out that you made
certain choices while building

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:53.950
your theory, and then
you find that this just

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:54.940
didn't work, right?

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.960
And that's experience for you.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.810
And then you use this
experience to potentially take

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:04.570
completely new directions.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:07.930
And then another piece
that is very important--

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:11.380
has been very important to the
history of math and science,

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:15.850
has been to discover
abstract concepts and tools.

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:19.990
So, for example, the
idea of calculus,

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:24.160
that really sped up the
progress of science after it

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:25.630
was invented.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:29.230
Similarly with
algebra and, really,

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:33.670
all major branches of
mathematics, came from the need

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:38.120
to abstract some sort
of real-world process.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.450
And then, the value
of these abstractions

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:44.200
is that they speed up both
this process of exploration

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:48.580
and also this learning process
that I previously mentioned.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:53.120
Now, if you think of
it, LLMs bring something

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.600
to the table regarding
each of these criteria.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.680
So, for example,
if you are doing

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:06.470
a search in a very large
space of potential scientific

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:11.240
hypotheses, and LLM has
inside it a lot of knowledge

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:16.700
about what sort of
hypotheses have historically

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:18.600
tended to work in science.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.400
And so, this knowledge
can potentially

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.140
help you direct
the search process.

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:32.240
And then, also in
this course, you

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:35.100
have seen examples
of LLM agents.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:38.030
And in this class, you are
going to see more of this.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.730
So these are
systems that contain

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:42.780
inside them LLMs as modules.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:44.720
But they are also
interacting with the world,

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:46.980
and they are learning
from experience.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:50.770
And this experience
is gathered as data

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:54.350
and then often stuck
into the prompt.

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:57.070
But sometimes you can even
do reinforcement learning

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:01.300
or other kinds of modification
of the LLM's weights.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:03.640
But the point is that there
are all these established

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.610
methods for doing
learning combined

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:08.710
with the use of language
models, and that

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:12.730
is something that we can
exploit here as well.

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:16.130
Now the use of LLMs
for abstraction,

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:20.210
this is something that's
not well explored yet.

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:23.830
This is a really
new idea, but I'm

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:28.420
going to give you some evidence
as to why there might be

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:31.180
a lot of things going on here.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:34.180
And there is
certainly a lot more

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:37.900
to be done about the use
of LLMs in constructing

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:40.930
new concepts and new tools.

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:48.470
So I think that LLM agents have
tremendous, endless promise

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:51.090
in mathematical and
scientific discovery.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:59.690
And the reason is that, so
far as all of these ideas go,

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:05.170
LLMs have a potential to
contribute with regards to each.

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:10.680
So this may all seem
somewhat abstract.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:14.670
So I'm going to now jump
into some concrete results.

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:18.030
And this lecture is
divided into two pieces.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.840
In the first part, we are
going to talk about AI

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:22.500
for mathematical discovery.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.500
And then in the
second part, we are

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:27.720
going to talk about AI
for scientific discovery.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:29.450
And in each of these
cases, I'm going

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:36.710
to show you some LLM
agents built using

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:38.400
quite different algorithms.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:46.160
But we'll see that these agents
realize many of these ideas

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:49.750
that we talked about
in the previous slide.

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:53.530
OK, so let's talk
about AI for math.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:58.000
Over the last few years,
there's been tremendous interest

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.060
in this field.

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:02.680
Here's Terry Tao
from 2023, talking

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:06.640
about how AI from
that year can already

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:10.150
generate suggestive
hints and help

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:11.810
with the mathematical process.

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:15.700
And he predicted
that by 2026, AI

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:18.070
can become a
trustworthy co-author

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:23.140
in math research papers and
in other fields as well.

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:27.250
Now, 2026 is not yet
here, but I would

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:34.180
say that this prediction seems
very plausible as of now.

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:38.440
Now, in terms of
concrete systems,

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:43.810
o1, when it was introduced,
one of the highlights

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:48.290
was that it did really well in
various competition mathematics

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:49.320
problems.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:52.050
So here is a plot.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:55.590
It shows how o1 does
compare to GPT-4o.

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:58.940
And as you see, accuracy in
this particular competition,

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:05.210
AIME 2024, has gone
up very substantially.

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:08.420
And then, of course,
last year, my colleagues

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:11.900
at Google DeepMind,
they built this system

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:16.020
that achieved silver medal-level
performance at the IMO.

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:20.460
And you've already heard a
talk about that in this course.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:23.990
So I'm not going to go
into further detail--

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:26.310
or to much more detail
about that in this talk,

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:30.230
but you know that
it's impressive,

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:32.940
and it uses a lot of very
impressive machinery.

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:42.200
Now, one question that I
think is important to ask

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:46.853
is that is it all going
to be done just with LLMs?

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:55.270
In other words, do you need any
sort of additional machinery

00:10:55.270 --> 00:10:59.810
that combines LLMs with,
for example, actions?

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:02.170
This is really
the characteristic

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:07.810
that distinguishes LLM
agents from just basic LLMs.

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:14.620
And so, I would say that there
are certainly still believers

00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:18.430
in the potential
of just LLMs alone

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:23.110
to do everything after
being trained on language.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:26.650
But I am personally a
little bit skeptical

00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:28.780
about that possibility.

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:33.850
Now, that said, one must admit
that over the last couple

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:36.940
of years, just LLMs
have come a long way.

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:43.310
So here is the picture of
how you train an LLM on--

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:45.600
for these sorts of
mathematical settings.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:48.150
So you're going to start
with a pre-trained LLM.

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:52.550
Then you're going to train it on
math-related web docs and then

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:56.150
problems with step-by-step
solutions, problems

00:11:56.150 --> 00:11:58.680
with tool-integrated solutions,
and so on and so forth.

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:02.310
And then, ultimately, you
have this LLM that can use,

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:07.790
for example, Python APIs and
that can solve math problems.

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:12.980
And this has, again, as
I said, come a long way.

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:16.790
So, for example, here is a
problem from the Putnam Math

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:18.690
Competition from last year.

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:22.340
And o1's response
on this problem

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:24.890
is actually very reasonable.

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:30.380
And as these systems are
getting better and better,

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:34.340
it seems like they're solving
more and more problems that

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:39.620
are of this nature that are
similarly hard-- or harder.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:44.500
But that said, there are
some fundamental weaknesses

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:47.440
of this neural-only
approach that you'd

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:50.350
get with just a basic LLM.

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:53.680
And the reason is that--

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:55.810
or one fundamental
reason is that there

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:59.410
is this issue of data scarcity.

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:04.000
In order to do rigorous
mathematical reasoning, not just

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:07.730
computing answers to
well-posed questions,

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:12.580
you need to have a lot
of high-quality traces

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:15.020
of mathematical reasoning.

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:17.320
Or, alternatively, you would
need high-quality reward

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:18.470
functions.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:23.230
And this is difficult to get
just with human-produced data

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:27.220
beyond high school or
competition settings.

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:29.470
And then a second
reason, fundamentally,

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:33.010
is that natural language
reasoning is hard to verify.

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:38.870
And this is something that
is a fundamental weakness

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:42.140
of these language-only models.

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:44.780
And there are even
settings where

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:47.060
you can apply this sort
of mathematical reasoning

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:48.643
where, for example,
when you are doing

00:13:48.643 --> 00:13:53.420
mathematical reasoning
about real-world systems

00:13:53.420 --> 00:14:00.000
and the behaviors of these
systems have real consequences,

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.000
so edge cases are
really critical.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.250
So in these cases you want
your mathematical reasoning

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:07.140
to be really robust
and rigorous.

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:13.160
And unfortunately,
language-based, purely neural

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:18.830
mathematical reasoning
tends to not be this way.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:22.340
And so, it is entirely
possible that scaling is going

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:24.600
to solve all of these problems.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:28.740
But as I said, I'm a little
bit skeptical about this.

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:32.780
So in this talk, I'm
going to demonstrate

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:37.030
an alternative strategy,
which is similar in many ways

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:41.200
to AlphaProof, which
you saw previously,

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:44.950
but which is more of
an LLM agent approach

00:14:44.950 --> 00:14:51.310
that is an alternative to this
sort of purely neural, purely

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:53.793
language-driven kind of model.

00:14:56.530 --> 00:15:01.510
So this alternative involves
formal representations, just

00:15:01.510 --> 00:15:03.310
like in AlphaProof.

00:15:03.310 --> 00:15:06.370
So there's an entire
literature that

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:11.210
is building up that follows
this kind of strategy.

00:15:11.210 --> 00:15:16.120
And so here, what you do is
that rather than just operating

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:17.860
in this space of
informal problem

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:21.970
statements and informal
solutions, you would be,

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:25.898
first, doing what is
called autoformalization.

00:15:25.898 --> 00:15:27.940
So you're going to go from
the informal statement

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:32.620
to a formal statement in a
language, like Lean or Coq,

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:34.210
or Isabelle.

00:15:34.210 --> 00:15:38.400
And lean, in particular, is
what is used for AlphaProof.

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:41.460
But there are other
frameworks like this as well

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:43.925
that have a very long history.

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:50.820
Now, the appeal of these
sorts of formal frameworks

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:55.390
is that a proof can be
written in a step-by-step way,

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:56.500
just like code.

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:59.490
And not only that, a
proof can be checked.

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:03.780
You can check if a proof
that you have written

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:09.840
in this formal language actually
implies a certain property

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:12.750
that you started out
with, so this theorem

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:14.700
that you started out with.

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:18.490
And so, the idea is that you
would then, in the second phase,

00:16:18.490 --> 00:16:20.593
take this formal
problem statement,

00:16:20.593 --> 00:16:23.010
and then you would be feeding
it to a neural prover, which

00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:25.780
is going to come up with
this sequence of steps,

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:30.700
which are also called tactics,
these rules that you apply.

00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:35.120
So these are tactics, and
you apply them one by one.

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:40.690
And then at the end, you
get to this QED stage,

00:16:40.690 --> 00:16:45.370
where you have
nothing left to prove.

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:48.590
And so that's how this
process would work.

00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:53.080
And the advantage here is that
if you think of the verification

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:55.930
challenge that I
mentioned earlier,

00:16:55.930 --> 00:17:00.832
where you want to check whether
a claimed proof is actually

00:17:00.832 --> 00:17:02.290
a proof, this is
something that you

00:17:02.290 --> 00:17:08.372
can establish relatively easily
using a proof assistant, right?

00:17:08.372 --> 00:17:12.099
And in terms of the data
issues, so the appeal

00:17:12.099 --> 00:17:16.880
of this kind of verifiability
is that during training time,

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:21.550
you can synthetically generate
a very, very large number

00:17:21.550 --> 00:17:24.040
of candidate proofs.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:27.099
And then you can immediately
get feedback on whether or not

00:17:27.099 --> 00:17:29.590
these proofs are correct.

00:17:29.590 --> 00:17:34.650
So this turns this
difficult problem of proof

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:38.160
into this kind of a
game-playing task, which

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:41.220
you can approach with really
a version of reinforcement

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:41.980
learning.

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:44.680
And this is what, for
example, AlphaProof did.

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:47.700
Although I'll show
here that you can also

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:53.130
take a more LLM-based
approach to this problem.

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:59.100
And in fact, you can build up
on these pre-trained language

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:02.680
models and only have a pretty
thin layer on top of that.

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:07.920
And that still does
quite interestingly well.

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:08.680
OK.

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:12.030
So just to go into a
little bit more detail

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:13.720
about these formal
representations,

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:19.170
even though you may have seen
this in the AlphaProof lecture,

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:23.170
I'll just quickly recap--

00:18:23.170 --> 00:18:24.640
recapitulate this.

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:29.130
So here is a theorem
that we are--

00:18:29.130 --> 00:18:30.830
let's say, we are
trying to prove.

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:33.950
And what this says
is that if x is even,

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:36.110
then x square is even as well.

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:38.320
So the theorem
statement over here

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:41.690
says that x is a natural number.

00:18:41.690 --> 00:18:47.410
And then it says that x
being even implies x square

00:18:47.410 --> 00:18:48.830
is also even.

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:50.260
So modulo 2, it's--

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:53.470
the remainder is zero.

00:18:53.470 --> 00:18:55.690
So now, in order
to prove this, you

00:18:55.690 --> 00:18:59.060
are going to apply this
sequence of tactics,

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:00.590
as I said previously.

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:08.020
Now, it's important to note
that the system, that the proof

00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:13.150
assistant, it has a
certain state that

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:15.940
depends on what the
original theorem is

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:19.360
and which tactics you
have applied so far.

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:21.440
So, for example,
in the beginning,

00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:25.730
you would have a state
that looks like this.

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:28.580
Now, just to spell this
out, what this means,

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:31.650
it says that there is one
goal that you currently

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:36.450
need to prove, and that goal is
that under this context, which

00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:41.280
is that x is a natural number,
this is the variable that you're

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:42.250
allowed to use.

00:19:42.250 --> 00:19:45.240
And then this symbol,
turnstile, means that this

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:46.780
is what you need to prove.

00:19:46.780 --> 00:19:51.030
So this is basically the theorem
that you're starting out with

00:19:51.030 --> 00:19:53.580
before you have
applied that tactic.

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:56.160
Now, you have this
state, and then

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:59.520
you apply the tactic, which is,
you can think of it if you're

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:03.100
thinking of this as a
reinforcement learning setup,

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:05.410
you are applying an action.

00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:09.540
And so, you apply that action,
and that leads to a new state.

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:12.730
And then this state
here looks like this.

00:20:12.730 --> 00:20:14.670
So this intro
tactic, what it does

00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:19.710
is that it introduces
this new hypothesis, which

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:23.160
over here now says
that, OK, let us assume

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:26.220
that x is even along with--

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:26.820
sorry.

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:29.680
Yeah x is even along with
x being a natural number.

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:32.030
And this property
that x is even,

00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:33.770
we are going to
give it a name, h.

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:38.020
And then under this context,
which is really like,

00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:40.130
you have now declared
a new variable

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:43.300
which has a certain
type attached to it.

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:46.840
So now you are trying to show
that this x squared is even

00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:48.160
as well.

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:51.130
And then you apply
this next tactic,

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:54.560
which leads to another state.

00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:56.950
So these tactics are
written by humans

00:20:56.950 --> 00:21:03.410
and they encode various rules of
simplification in mathematics.

00:21:03.410 --> 00:21:05.380
And so what you
are really doing is

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:08.510
that for each of these rewrite
rules that you are applying,

00:21:08.510 --> 00:21:10.370
the state is changing.

00:21:10.370 --> 00:21:12.190
So, for example, now
the state has changed

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:16.450
to you showing that if--

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:21.670
so this x modulo 2 times x
modulo 2, the whole thing modulo

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:23.390
2, that is equal to zero.

00:21:23.390 --> 00:21:27.340
And then the next rewrite
is going to simplify this.

00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:29.070
And then, finally,
you will end up

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:33.810
with zero equals zero basically.

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:36.240
And at that point,
you're going to hit QED

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:39.040
because your goal will
just disappear, right?

00:21:39.040 --> 00:21:40.590
So each of these
rules you can think

00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:41.920
of as simplifying the state.

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:45.240
And then at the end, ideally
you want to end up in this state

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:47.320
where there are no open goals.

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:51.540
And at that point,
you can end the proof.

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:55.090
So that's what these formal
proof assistants look like.

00:21:55.090 --> 00:21:59.440
Really, they're state machines
where you have the proof state.

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:01.630
And then these actions,
which are these tactics.

00:22:01.630 --> 00:22:03.910
And then they lead
to a new proof state.

00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:08.250
And your goal really is to
find a sequence of tactics

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:14.970
that take you from this initial
state of the prover to this QED

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:18.076
state, where there are no
more goals to be discharged.

00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:26.860
OK, so now let's talk
about the two phases

00:22:26.860 --> 00:22:28.340
that I mentioned previously.

00:22:28.340 --> 00:22:32.090
The autoformalization phase
is something like this.

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:34.810
You are starting with this
informal math statement.

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:36.920
There exists an infinite
number of primes.

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:40.690
And then there is this
proof of this as well in

00:22:40.690 --> 00:22:41.810
natural language.

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:45.410
And now you are applying
your autoformalization model.

00:22:45.410 --> 00:22:48.670
And later I'll talk a little bit
about how this could be done.

00:22:48.670 --> 00:22:51.430
But the version 0
of this would be

00:22:51.430 --> 00:22:55.520
that you throw a language model
at this informal statement,

00:22:55.520 --> 00:22:58.310
and then out comes this
formal theorem, right?

00:22:58.310 --> 00:23:01.030
And so this one is in Lean.

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:05.200
Now what this says is that--

00:23:05.200 --> 00:23:09.320
well, I won't read
this thing in detail,

00:23:09.320 --> 00:23:13.130
but maybe I can read
the theorem statement.

00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:16.330
It says that, so for
all n there exists

00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:20.180
a p such that p is bigger than--
greater than or equal to n,

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:22.093
and then p is also
a prime, right?

00:23:22.093 --> 00:23:23.760
So that's how you
establish that there's

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:25.420
an infinite number of primes.

00:23:25.420 --> 00:23:27.800
And then the rest
of it is the proof.

00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:33.210
So then, let's
assume that you have

00:23:33.210 --> 00:23:34.740
done this sort of
formalization, you

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:37.320
have a formal
statement of a theorem,

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:39.400
and now you're
trying to prove it.

00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:43.270
So previously, I showed you
that there are these tactics,

00:23:43.270 --> 00:23:45.460
these simplification rules
that you could apply.

00:23:45.460 --> 00:23:49.110
But now you can imagine a search
problem where at each state

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:53.320
there is a set of potential
tactics that you could apply.

00:23:53.320 --> 00:23:56.320
And this leads to the
creation of a search tree.

00:23:56.320 --> 00:23:58.560
And then your goal
is to basically find

00:23:58.560 --> 00:24:03.000
a path in this tree
that ends up at QED.

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:07.230
Now, of course, the complexity
of here is that at each point

00:24:07.230 --> 00:24:11.190
you can have a very, very large
number of potential branches

00:24:11.190 --> 00:24:12.390
in this tree.

00:24:12.390 --> 00:24:15.220
And then, also, this tree
can be quite deep, right?

00:24:15.220 --> 00:24:17.610
Because if you're talking
about really advanced

00:24:17.610 --> 00:24:21.580
mathematical theorems,
you could end up

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:24.950
with proofs that are thousands
and thousands of lines long.

00:24:24.950 --> 00:24:27.490
And so, this is a
far harder problem

00:24:27.490 --> 00:24:29.380
than any game-playing
problem that we

00:24:29.380 --> 00:24:33.040
have been able to solve so far.

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:36.700
So it's remarkable to see
the kind of progress that

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:39.975
has already happened in this
task over the last few years.

00:24:42.520 --> 00:24:45.910
Now, AlphaProof, of course,
you have already seen.

00:24:45.910 --> 00:24:47.680
You've seen this picture.

00:24:47.680 --> 00:24:50.680
There's this formalizer network
that does this formalization

00:24:50.680 --> 00:24:52.160
into formal problems.

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:54.040
And then the solver
network, it tries

00:24:54.040 --> 00:24:56.740
to generate these formal proofs.

00:24:56.740 --> 00:25:00.490
One big insight was that
even more formalized problems

00:25:00.490 --> 00:25:02.090
are still helpful for learning.

00:25:02.090 --> 00:25:06.130
And this sort of proof
the solver network this

00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:07.700
is being trained with RL.

00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:11.110
And then, one big insight was
that you could complement RL

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:13.930
training with test-time RL when
you're actually doing the IMO

00:25:13.930 --> 00:25:18.420
or any other-- solving any other
math problem you could would

00:25:18.420 --> 00:25:19.770
generate--

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:25.410
you could basically do this
sort of test-time RL on that.

00:25:25.410 --> 00:25:27.610
But again, you have
seen all of this.

00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:29.460
So I'll move on.

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:32.610
The approach that I'm
going to talk about today,

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:34.750
this is called COPRA.

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:39.210
This was published in a paper
at the Conference of Language

00:25:39.210 --> 00:25:41.460
Models last year.

00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:43.890
And this is led
by my PhD student

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:47.430
at UT Austin, Amitayush Thakur.

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:53.130
Now, this is the overall
picture of COPRA.

00:25:53.130 --> 00:26:00.420
It still has this view of
interaction between an LLM

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:04.440
that is predicting
the proof steps

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:08.400
and the actual prover
that is executing

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:12.750
the step that is
proposed by the LLM

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:16.080
and then giving you a new state.

00:26:16.080 --> 00:26:19.150
However, the difference
is that while AlphaProof

00:26:19.150 --> 00:26:24.040
does this sort of advanced
reinforcement learning

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:24.710
for training.

00:26:24.710 --> 00:26:27.610
So here, you're just taking
this sort of an LLM agent

00:26:27.610 --> 00:26:32.170
approach, where you
have a frontier LLM,

00:26:32.170 --> 00:26:35.440
and the frontier LLM is
being provided the proof

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:40.520
state and some information
from the history of the search

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:41.420
so far.

00:26:41.420 --> 00:26:45.760
And then it is
making a prediction

00:26:45.760 --> 00:26:48.800
as to what's the next
step that you should take.

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:51.740
And then you are taking that
step, and you are executing it,

00:26:51.740 --> 00:26:55.880
and then you are getting back
a new state of the proof.

00:26:55.880 --> 00:26:58.960
And then this process
is continuing, right?

00:26:58.960 --> 00:27:00.850
Now one important
thing is that there

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:03.910
is an external search that is
going on, a depth-first search.

00:27:03.910 --> 00:27:07.060
And then inside the depth-first
search, you are doing all this,

00:27:07.060 --> 00:27:07.580
right?

00:27:07.580 --> 00:27:10.030
But it is fundamentally
different in the sense

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:12.200
that everything is in context.

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:17.340
There is no actual adjustment
to the weights happening.

00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:19.200
But what is interesting
is that even

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:21.340
with a relatively small
amount of compute,

00:27:21.340 --> 00:27:24.280
this approach was still
able to do quite well.

00:27:24.280 --> 00:27:26.400
And so, I think
that this strategy

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:29.820
has a lot of long-term promise.

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:34.360
For one, because it immediately
leverages advances in LLMs,

00:27:34.360 --> 00:27:40.350
as, for example, you go
from GPT-4 to o1 to o3

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:44.640
and to maybe someday
GPT-5, then you're

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:49.830
going to immediately leverage
those advantages-- advances.

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:54.210
And it also has this ability
to integrate natural language

00:27:54.210 --> 00:27:58.650
and formal language reasoning,
because ultimately, you

00:27:58.650 --> 00:28:01.860
are just using an
LLM to generate

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:05.460
the proofs in the formal--

00:28:05.460 --> 00:28:08.230
as formal language,
but at the same time,

00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:11.590
the LLM has the ability to
do natural language as well.

00:28:11.590 --> 00:28:14.380
So this is something
you can benefit from.

00:28:14.380 --> 00:28:17.290
And I'm going to show
concrete examples of this

00:28:17.290 --> 00:28:19.540
in a little bit.

00:28:19.540 --> 00:28:21.670
Another thing is
that it's also--

00:28:21.670 --> 00:28:24.130
this strategy is also
applicable even if there is

00:28:24.130 --> 00:28:26.470
no corpus of training problems.

00:28:26.470 --> 00:28:30.620
Now, one of the challenges
in doing AI for math,

00:28:30.620 --> 00:28:34.730
once you go outside of settings
such as math competitions,

00:28:34.730 --> 00:28:37.877
is that you're really trying
to solve a new problem, right?

00:28:37.877 --> 00:28:39.460
So if you're a math
researcher, you're

00:28:39.460 --> 00:28:43.300
trying to solve a new problem
that, by design, is quite

00:28:43.300 --> 00:28:46.390
different from the problems
that a lot of other people

00:28:46.390 --> 00:28:48.460
have solved previously.

00:28:48.460 --> 00:28:52.780
So in such a situation,
it's a bit challenging

00:28:52.780 --> 00:28:58.380
to create a corpus
of training problems

00:28:58.380 --> 00:29:03.440
and then do training
of an RL agent.

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:07.340
However, with an
in-context learning agent,

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:12.770
the appeal is that
you can still use it.

00:29:12.770 --> 00:29:16.250
The LLM has inside it a
ton of prior knowledge,

00:29:16.250 --> 00:29:19.630
and that knowledge is something
that you could benefit from,

00:29:19.630 --> 00:29:20.130
right?

00:29:20.130 --> 00:29:22.600
And the LLM can, for
example, bring in knowledge

00:29:22.600 --> 00:29:26.050
about related problems,
even though it's not

00:29:26.050 --> 00:29:31.660
been explicitly trained on them
beyond the pre-training stage.

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:36.250
And in the longer run, of
course, the two approaches

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:41.660
of in-context learning
and explicit training,

00:29:41.660 --> 00:29:43.280
these are not incompatible.

00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:46.030
So you could imagine the
in-context learning agent being

00:29:46.030 --> 00:29:51.190
used to generate a lot
of different traces

00:29:51.190 --> 00:29:53.510
by interaction with
the proof assistant,

00:29:53.510 --> 00:29:57.820
and then you could use
them for doing fine-tuning.

00:29:57.820 --> 00:30:00.820
Now, one question that
you may have over here

00:30:00.820 --> 00:30:04.690
is that, well, so
do I really need

00:30:04.690 --> 00:30:06.260
to even have Lean in that case?

00:30:06.260 --> 00:30:10.580
Couldn't I just rely
on the LLM alone?

00:30:10.580 --> 00:30:12.320
And I would say that
the difference here

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:16.880
is that this interaction
with the proof assistant

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:21.180
is still providing a very
strong form of grounding.

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:25.490
So, for example, if you take
a step predicted by the LLM

00:30:25.490 --> 00:30:28.980
and then you find that this
is actually an invalid step,

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:30.920
you're going to get
that feedback right away

00:30:30.920 --> 00:30:32.370
from the proof assistant.

00:30:32.370 --> 00:30:35.990
And you can take that feedback,
and you can then stick it back

00:30:35.990 --> 00:30:37.830
into the prompt of the LLM.

00:30:37.830 --> 00:30:42.295
And then you can
ask it to continue.

00:30:42.295 --> 00:30:44.420
And then, hopefully, it
will learn from its mistake

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:45.966
and carry on, right?

00:30:45.966 --> 00:30:48.770
So in the long
run, it's possible

00:30:48.770 --> 00:30:51.920
that once you have generated a
very, very, very large number

00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:55.250
of traces via interaction
with the prover,

00:30:55.250 --> 00:30:58.830
maybe you have fine-tuned your
LLM on this-- all this data,

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:00.230
and then the LLM
is progressively

00:31:00.230 --> 00:31:01.620
getting better and better.

00:31:01.620 --> 00:31:06.230
And there is a point
beyond which it just

00:31:06.230 --> 00:31:09.640
doesn't make any mistakes
with this kind of proof.

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:14.320
Maybe we'll get there someday,
but we are still pretty far away

00:31:14.320 --> 00:31:16.750
from that possibility.

00:31:16.750 --> 00:31:22.930
So at least, as of now, I think
that this sort of interaction

00:31:22.930 --> 00:31:28.360
between LLM and proof assistant
is still extremely valuable.

00:31:28.360 --> 00:31:32.140
OK, so here is this COPRA system
in a little bit more detail.

00:31:32.140 --> 00:31:33.760
So what is going
on here is that you

00:31:33.760 --> 00:31:36.400
are starting with
a formal theorem,

00:31:36.400 --> 00:31:38.990
but you could also have
some informal hints.

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:42.730
You could imagine bringing
autoformalization into this

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:44.860
picture as well, but we didn't.

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:47.230
And so, the first
step is that you

00:31:47.230 --> 00:31:51.310
create a prompt based on the
statement of the theorem.

00:31:51.310 --> 00:31:58.660
And then you feed it
to a frontier LLM,

00:31:58.660 --> 00:32:04.150
and you just ask it to generate
some potential tactics that

00:32:04.150 --> 00:32:05.690
would be of interest.

00:32:05.690 --> 00:32:12.950
And now you do
the, your model has

00:32:12.950 --> 00:32:18.410
predicted a set of one of
the actions, or tactics,

00:32:18.410 --> 00:32:19.230
in this case.

00:32:19.230 --> 00:32:21.620
And then you parse
that tactic because it

00:32:21.620 --> 00:32:22.800
will be in the form of text.

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:25.650
So now you parse it into
a structured format.

00:32:25.650 --> 00:32:31.110
And then you just execute it
via the proof environment.

00:32:31.110 --> 00:32:35.460
And this could be Lean
or Coq or Isabelle.

00:32:35.460 --> 00:32:39.110
And then after you do
that, in the lucky case,

00:32:39.110 --> 00:32:42.540
you already hit QED,
and this is all done.

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:49.370
But if you fail, if
this tactic is invalid,

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:53.750
or there are still some
open goals that you still

00:32:53.750 --> 00:32:57.870
need to resolve, in that
case, you get some feedback,

00:32:57.870 --> 00:33:03.950
and then you augment the prompt
and then backtrack if needed.

00:33:03.950 --> 00:33:07.440
And then, we also have
this retrieval phase

00:33:07.440 --> 00:33:13.810
over here, which seem to add to
the performance of the system.

00:33:13.810 --> 00:33:17.340
And here, you are also able
to query a lemma database

00:33:17.340 --> 00:33:19.900
and bring in some
relevant lemmas

00:33:19.900 --> 00:33:21.760
and then add it
back to the prompt.

00:33:21.760 --> 00:33:24.510
And this process just
continues over and over

00:33:24.510 --> 00:33:29.830
and over again until you have
resolved the original theorem,

00:33:29.830 --> 00:33:33.060
or alternatively,
you have timed out.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:36.750
So here's the prompt of the
system in a little bit more

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:37.660
detail.

00:33:37.660 --> 00:33:42.850
So here, as you see, there is
the serialized proof state.

00:33:42.850 --> 00:33:46.050
And here, you have
what is the goal

00:33:46.050 --> 00:33:47.620
that you still have to prove.

00:33:47.620 --> 00:33:52.660
And what are the hypotheses
that are being made.

00:33:52.660 --> 00:33:55.502
So over here, for example,
the hypothesis include--

00:33:55.502 --> 00:33:57.460
these are the assumptions
you're making, right?

00:33:57.460 --> 00:34:01.690
So this includes x being of
type NAT, natural numbers.

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:05.060
And then, also, x being even.

00:34:05.060 --> 00:34:07.250
And then you have
the goal, which

00:34:07.250 --> 00:34:11.510
is x square modulo
2 is equal to 0.

00:34:11.510 --> 00:34:15.900
We also have this information
about the last step.

00:34:15.900 --> 00:34:18.110
So the way to read this is
that from left to right,

00:34:18.110 --> 00:34:21.449
you are looking at successive
queries to the LLM.

00:34:21.449 --> 00:34:24.800
So the first column
over here, this

00:34:24.800 --> 00:34:27.380
includes information about
what is the first query

00:34:27.380 --> 00:34:28.440
that you are making.

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:30.239
So the first thing
that you have done.

00:34:30.239 --> 00:34:32.840
So let's say that
you already have

00:34:32.840 --> 00:34:38.120
made this decision that you
are going to have this intro h

00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:38.790
tactic, right?

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:41.659
So that's what created
that hypothesis, x squared

00:34:41.659 --> 00:34:43.370
modulo 2 equals 0.

00:34:43.370 --> 00:34:45.870
So then this is the last step.

00:34:45.870 --> 00:34:52.370
This is stored in a stack and
fed as part of the prompt.

00:34:52.370 --> 00:34:57.560
Now then, at this point
there has been no error.

00:34:57.560 --> 00:35:02.690
So then, you get this
response from this LLM

00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:06.300
after you feed this information
that the next thing that you

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:08.190
should do is RWH.

00:35:08.190 --> 00:35:11.680
And then, now, if you
try to execute it,

00:35:11.680 --> 00:35:15.690
you might get an error message
because the rewrite tactic

00:35:15.690 --> 00:35:16.540
failed.

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:21.160
And then this information,
this error feedback,

00:35:21.160 --> 00:35:23.970
you send it back to
the LLM, and then

00:35:23.970 --> 00:35:27.010
that's the next You say
that, try again LLM.

00:35:27.010 --> 00:35:30.510
And then the LLM says
that, OK, let's try apply

00:35:30.510 --> 00:35:33.270
nat.mul_mod_right.

00:35:33.270 --> 00:35:35.650
Now the details of this
tactic doesn't matter,

00:35:35.650 --> 00:35:38.740
it's something involving
modular multiplication.

00:35:38.740 --> 00:35:40.720
But the point is
this is also wrong.

00:35:40.720 --> 00:35:42.240
So then, again,
at the next step,

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:44.370
you get an error feedback--

00:35:44.370 --> 00:35:45.430
error message.

00:35:45.430 --> 00:35:48.700
And this is something you
stick back into the prompt.

00:35:48.700 --> 00:35:50.680
And then you make a query again.

00:35:50.680 --> 00:35:56.500
And now it says, oh, let's try
this rewrite with nat.mul_mod.

00:35:56.500 --> 00:35:58.690
And, yay, this time it works.

00:35:58.690 --> 00:36:01.860
So now you see that the
interaction result doesn't

00:36:01.860 --> 00:36:03.130
have any error message in it.

00:36:03.130 --> 00:36:04.360
It's a success.

00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:08.950
And so, this is now
put into the stack.

00:36:08.950 --> 00:36:11.290
And so the last step,
as you see, has changed.

00:36:11.290 --> 00:36:14.710
It's now rw nat.mul_mod.

00:36:14.710 --> 00:36:18.820
So now you're going to try
to generate the next step.

00:36:18.820 --> 00:36:24.250
And then this time it-- the
LLM generates this rw h, right.

00:36:24.250 --> 00:36:26.680
And so this is how this
process is going to continue.

00:36:26.680 --> 00:36:34.530
In a nutshell, you may query
the LLM, get some error message,

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:36.780
and then stick it
back into the prompt,

00:36:36.780 --> 00:36:38.260
and then continue, right?

00:36:38.260 --> 00:36:42.750
And this process
would just go on.

00:36:42.750 --> 00:36:44.031
OK.

00:36:44.031 --> 00:36:51.460
So this evaluation was done
in 2024, actually late 2023.

00:36:51.460 --> 00:36:53.890
So the world has
changed since then.

00:36:53.890 --> 00:36:56.730
But at least at that point,
we compared with GPT-4

00:36:56.730 --> 00:37:01.530
and found that GPT-4 frequently
gave incorrect errors,

00:37:01.530 --> 00:37:04.230
incorrect proofs.

00:37:04.230 --> 00:37:11.490
And by contrast, this feedback
with the proof environment,

00:37:11.490 --> 00:37:15.460
Lean in this case,
really helped for COPRA.

00:37:15.460 --> 00:37:19.240
And so, even though it was
using the same underlying model,

00:37:19.240 --> 00:37:22.410
the interaction allowed it
to create a correct proof

00:37:22.410 --> 00:37:23.225
in this case.

00:37:25.920 --> 00:37:27.790
Here are some
experimental results.

00:37:27.790 --> 00:37:30.880
You can see the
details in the paper.

00:37:30.880 --> 00:37:33.370
But in this picture,
on the x-axis,

00:37:33.370 --> 00:37:34.990
you see the number of queries.

00:37:34.990 --> 00:37:38.230
And on the y-axis, you see
the number of theorems proved.

00:37:38.230 --> 00:37:43.770
So what we saw over here is
that, pretty quickly, the COPRA

00:37:43.770 --> 00:37:46.170
approach was able to
prove a lot of theorems

00:37:46.170 --> 00:37:48.840
with a relatively small
number of queries.

00:37:48.840 --> 00:37:55.040
Whereas the other approaches,
that didn't get that far.

00:37:57.750 --> 00:38:01.190
And here is an evaluation
on miniF2F test.

00:38:01.190 --> 00:38:04.410
So the absolute
numbers are not great.

00:38:04.410 --> 00:38:06.350
But then the amount
of compute we

00:38:06.350 --> 00:38:09.930
provided COPRA was
also relatively small.

00:38:09.930 --> 00:38:14.490
We just allowed 60
queries per proof attempt.

00:38:14.490 --> 00:38:21.530
Now what we saw here was
that we got quite reasonable

00:38:21.530 --> 00:38:26.450
performance, comparable with
the state-of-the-art methods.

00:38:26.450 --> 00:38:31.790
Now, of course,
since then, the state

00:38:31.790 --> 00:38:34.040
of the art on this benchmark
has moved quite a bit.

00:38:34.040 --> 00:38:36.660
So miniF2F, for those of you
who don't know, is, by the way,

00:38:36.660 --> 00:38:41.000
it's a compilation of
computation mathematics

00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:44.960
problems, mostly at
the high school level.

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:51.230
And so it's of the form
that here is a theorem

00:38:51.230 --> 00:38:53.430
and come up with a lean proof.

00:38:53.430 --> 00:38:55.400
So here, what we
see is that there

00:38:55.400 --> 00:39:02.580
was this around 29% or
30% success rate, which

00:39:02.580 --> 00:39:05.137
is behind the current SOTA.

00:39:05.137 --> 00:39:06.720
But I think one
question would be that

00:39:06.720 --> 00:39:08.910
if you take this approach
and scale it up, then

00:39:08.910 --> 00:39:10.230
where would you end up?

00:39:10.230 --> 00:39:13.355
And there are no public
results on that yet.

00:39:16.110 --> 00:39:18.570
So in this talk,
though, I'm going

00:39:18.570 --> 00:39:26.140
to go beyond the method
that we just presented,

00:39:26.140 --> 00:39:32.460
which is the basic COPRA, and
talk about some results that we

00:39:32.460 --> 00:39:33.780
just recently developed.

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:35.940
This is not published yet.

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:42.450
And here, we are leveraging
the ability of LLMs

00:39:42.450 --> 00:39:49.110
to abstract and also to use
natural language reasoning,

00:39:49.110 --> 00:39:50.850
because I think
that this is really

00:39:50.850 --> 00:39:55.150
where the sort of approach
that we just presented.

00:39:55.150 --> 00:39:58.310
This shines because of
the tremendous amount

00:39:58.310 --> 00:40:00.840
of world knowledge that
is stored inside LLMs.

00:40:00.840 --> 00:40:07.370
We can really use them in a
lot more powerful capacity

00:40:07.370 --> 00:40:12.410
than just as a predictor
of the next tactic.

00:40:12.410 --> 00:40:16.520
And so, here's a sample problem.

00:40:16.520 --> 00:40:17.940
This is from IMO.

00:40:17.940 --> 00:40:21.500
And this is saying that,
prove that this fraction is

00:40:21.500 --> 00:40:24.890
irreducible for every
natural number n.

00:40:24.890 --> 00:40:32.000
So what we'll see is
that the COPRA approach

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:37.910
can be extended a little
bit to, in fact, come up

00:40:37.910 --> 00:40:42.050
with a hierarchical
solution to this problem.

00:40:42.050 --> 00:40:45.110
And so, this will involve
breaking down the problem

00:40:45.110 --> 00:40:48.560
into subproblems and then
solving hierarchically.

00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:50.340
OK, so how do we do this?

00:40:50.340 --> 00:40:52.490
Well, first what
we do is that we

00:40:52.490 --> 00:40:57.630
ask the LLM to come up
with an informal solution

00:40:57.630 --> 00:40:59.220
to this problem.

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:04.660
And so, I don't know if this
font is too small to read,

00:41:04.660 --> 00:41:08.800
but basically what is going on
here is that this was fed to 03,

00:41:08.800 --> 00:41:12.210
a reasoning model, and so, it
was able to come up with this

00:41:12.210 --> 00:41:14.190
step-by-step plan.

00:41:14.190 --> 00:41:17.580
So, for example, you
first define the GCD

00:41:17.580 --> 00:41:21.310
and then subtract to simplify.

00:41:21.310 --> 00:41:23.320
Then you apply the
Euclidean algorithm.

00:41:23.320 --> 00:41:25.570
And then you conclude
something about the GCD.

00:41:25.570 --> 00:41:29.700
And then you have
the final conclusion.

00:41:29.700 --> 00:41:33.510
So then, we say
that, OK, so let's

00:41:33.510 --> 00:41:39.100
take this informal decomposition
and put it in the context.

00:41:39.100 --> 00:41:43.440
Now, let's say
that it's our goal

00:41:43.440 --> 00:41:47.860
to prove this lemma
in Lean 4, right?

00:41:47.860 --> 00:41:50.880
So this is the formal
statement of the same problem.

00:41:50.880 --> 00:41:52.760
And we are just
saying that, don't

00:41:52.760 --> 00:41:54.550
try to write the proof, OK?

00:41:54.550 --> 00:41:59.540
So let's just write the subgoals
that are needed to prove this.

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:01.790
But we are doing
this conditioned

00:42:01.790 --> 00:42:06.530
on the informal proof
that we just generated.

00:42:06.530 --> 00:42:10.670
And so, here is the proof of--

00:42:10.670 --> 00:42:14.010
or here is the
decomposition of this lemma.

00:42:14.010 --> 00:42:21.090
So what you see is that it comes
up with these three subgoals.

00:42:21.090 --> 00:42:29.420
So first, you show that
this GCD step 1 is true.

00:42:29.420 --> 00:42:33.770
And it's basically saying that
this GCD of 21n plus 4 times 14n

00:42:33.770 --> 00:42:36.000
plus 3 is the same
as the GCD of this.

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:38.800
14n plus 3 times 7n plus 1.

00:42:42.080 --> 00:42:48.408
So then there is the second
one, and there's the third one,

00:42:48.408 --> 00:42:50.397
and so on and so forth.

00:42:50.397 --> 00:42:52.230
And then, finally, you
have the main theorem

00:42:52.230 --> 00:42:55.470
that combines all of the above.

00:42:55.470 --> 00:42:58.020
So the appeal of this
sort of decomposition

00:42:58.020 --> 00:43:02.970
is that now this
very complex problem

00:43:02.970 --> 00:43:07.050
has been simplified into
these three subproblems, which

00:43:07.050 --> 00:43:09.730
are a lot more tractable.

00:43:09.730 --> 00:43:12.960
So then in the
next step, we then

00:43:12.960 --> 00:43:17.700
ask COPRA to solve the
subproblems one by one.

00:43:17.700 --> 00:43:22.360
And what we see is
that COPRA is, in fact,

00:43:22.360 --> 00:43:26.980
able to come up with these
proofs of the subproblems.

00:43:26.980 --> 00:43:29.140
And here we are.

00:43:29.140 --> 00:43:30.450
Here are the proofs.

00:43:30.450 --> 00:43:33.580
Again, I'm not going to go
into the details of the proofs.

00:43:33.580 --> 00:43:37.630
This is something that you have
to stare at for a little bit.

00:43:37.630 --> 00:43:42.780
And it's not actually
especially complicated.

00:43:42.780 --> 00:43:48.520
But then, once you have
proven these lemmas,

00:43:48.520 --> 00:43:53.480
then you just put them
all in the context,

00:43:53.480 --> 00:43:56.840
and then you also put
in the informal proof,

00:43:56.840 --> 00:44:03.620
and then you ask Copra to
prove the full theorem.

00:44:03.620 --> 00:44:06.710
And it is able to do that.

00:44:06.710 --> 00:44:08.910
But COPRA is not
able to prove this

00:44:08.910 --> 00:44:15.050
if you were giving it just this
entire theorem all as one block

00:44:15.050 --> 00:44:18.710
and just asked it to solve.

00:44:18.710 --> 00:44:23.030
So what we did here
is really something

00:44:23.030 --> 00:44:32.348
that is a highly powerful
design pattern in LLM agents.

00:44:32.348 --> 00:44:35.600
LLM agents are
extraordinarily flexible.

00:44:35.600 --> 00:44:41.940
So with just some variations
on the ways you do prompting,

00:44:41.940 --> 00:44:44.120
you are able to make it
do very, very different

00:44:44.120 --> 00:44:47.740
things, things that would take
a tremendous amount of training

00:44:47.740 --> 00:44:50.650
if you weren't using
these pre-trained models.

00:44:50.650 --> 00:44:53.980
And so what we did
over here is that we

00:44:53.980 --> 00:44:56.890
started with a
system that was just

00:44:56.890 --> 00:45:02.800
designed to be a step-by-step
predictor of a low-level proof.

00:45:02.800 --> 00:45:05.560
And then we made it do
high-level reasoning

00:45:05.560 --> 00:45:07.190
at this more abstract
level, right,

00:45:07.190 --> 00:45:11.440
because if you can
think of these subgoals

00:45:11.440 --> 00:45:15.910
as basically being
abstractions of subproblems.

00:45:15.910 --> 00:45:20.680
And so the LLM is able to
create these abstractions.

00:45:20.680 --> 00:45:22.210
And once you have created--

00:45:22.210 --> 00:45:26.530
made use of these abstractions,
your proof process

00:45:26.530 --> 00:45:28.600
becomes a lot simpler,
because now you're

00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:31.480
able to go hierarchically.

00:45:31.480 --> 00:45:35.050
And all of this
was doable without

00:45:35.050 --> 00:45:37.390
any substantial training.

00:45:37.390 --> 00:45:43.540
You were just using
the power of prompting.

00:45:43.540 --> 00:45:47.370
And to me, this is
a huge revelation,

00:45:47.370 --> 00:45:52.260
the idea that you can use
the power of language,

00:45:52.260 --> 00:45:55.710
the power of all of the
stored knowledge inside LLMs,

00:45:55.710 --> 00:45:57.320
and then combine
that with this sort

00:45:57.320 --> 00:46:02.630
of higher-level algorithmics
where you just put together

00:46:02.630 --> 00:46:05.960
the informal and formal
reasoning steps in various ways

00:46:05.960 --> 00:46:07.620
and change the
prompting strategy,

00:46:07.620 --> 00:46:10.790
and you get something
that is qualitatively

00:46:10.790 --> 00:46:12.840
different in capability.

00:46:16.670 --> 00:46:19.910
And now it turns out that
the Lean feedback here

00:46:19.910 --> 00:46:22.580
was also really important
because at least

00:46:22.580 --> 00:46:25.400
with o3-mini, what we found is
that there were hallucinated

00:46:25.400 --> 00:46:26.610
lemmas and so on.

00:46:26.610 --> 00:46:30.980
And the grounding that was
provided by the Lean theorem

00:46:30.980 --> 00:46:35.350
prover was absolutely critical
to solve this problem.

00:46:37.940 --> 00:46:40.670
So now I want to
talk a little bit

00:46:40.670 --> 00:46:44.510
about a different
application of the same idea.

00:46:44.510 --> 00:46:47.290
This is in the context of formal
system verification, which

00:46:47.290 --> 00:46:51.580
is one of the most
practical applications

00:46:51.580 --> 00:46:54.580
of rigorous
mathematical reasoning.

00:46:54.580 --> 00:46:56.740
And so, what you're doing
in formal verification

00:46:56.740 --> 00:47:00.550
is that you are mathematically
modeling a real-world system

00:47:00.550 --> 00:47:04.930
as a set of definitions,
and then you

00:47:04.930 --> 00:47:07.270
are modeling system
properties as theorems

00:47:07.270 --> 00:47:08.690
involving these definitions.

00:47:08.690 --> 00:47:11.860
And then you're
proving these theorems.

00:47:11.860 --> 00:47:17.150
These methods have led to
extremely rigorous systems,

00:47:17.150 --> 00:47:19.120
systems that are
very difficult to--

00:47:19.120 --> 00:47:21.460
or almost impossible to hack.

00:47:21.460 --> 00:47:25.990
For example, there was this
time in a program organized

00:47:25.990 --> 00:47:31.120
by the US Department
of Defense, they

00:47:31.120 --> 00:47:39.160
used this formally verified
Linux kernel called seL4 to--

00:47:39.160 --> 00:47:41.870
and they put this on a drone.

00:47:41.870 --> 00:47:45.350
And then they got
a red team to try

00:47:45.350 --> 00:47:50.210
to attack this drone for
a period of six weeks,

00:47:50.210 --> 00:47:53.810
and they failed because
the rigor provided

00:47:53.810 --> 00:47:57.110
by formal verification
was just so much.

00:47:57.110 --> 00:47:59.660
Now, historically,
formal verification

00:47:59.660 --> 00:48:05.690
has been extremely expensive,
and as a result, it's costs--

00:48:05.690 --> 00:48:08.060
it's not really
been widely deployed

00:48:08.060 --> 00:48:12.050
because the costs are not
justifiable except in the most

00:48:12.050 --> 00:48:15.050
safety-critical scenarios.

00:48:15.050 --> 00:48:19.340
However, with the rise of
AI, especially AI for math,

00:48:19.340 --> 00:48:23.690
the cost-benefit equation
changes quite drastically.

00:48:23.690 --> 00:48:29.610
And now, you can imagine
doing not only proofs of math,

00:48:29.610 --> 00:48:32.570
but proofs of
real-world systems of,

00:48:32.570 --> 00:48:35.180
for example, correctness,
security, performance, and so

00:48:35.180 --> 00:48:41.380
on, using this growing
generation of AI tools.

00:48:41.380 --> 00:48:48.140
So here, I'm going to show you
a basic example of this idea.

00:48:48.140 --> 00:48:50.320
This can be scaled
up substantially

00:48:50.320 --> 00:48:56.680
with more powerful
models and more compute.

00:48:56.680 --> 00:48:59.020
But here, what we
are going to do

00:48:59.020 --> 00:49:02.110
is to give you a very,
very basic compiler

00:49:02.110 --> 00:49:07.420
and then show how to formally
verify its correctness.

00:49:07.420 --> 00:49:09.550
Now this is so
simple, the compiler,

00:49:09.550 --> 00:49:11.120
that it's almost a joke.

00:49:11.120 --> 00:49:17.330
But trust me, when you go to
more advanced compiler design,

00:49:17.330 --> 00:49:18.860
the fundamentals don't change.

00:49:18.860 --> 00:49:21.980
It's really the
same sort of idea,

00:49:21.980 --> 00:49:26.140
it's just that there
is more complexity.

00:49:26.140 --> 00:49:29.950
Now, OK, to do
compiler verification,

00:49:29.950 --> 00:49:31.760
you would need the
following pieces.

00:49:31.760 --> 00:49:38.200
So first of all, you would need
to have a notion of a language.

00:49:38.200 --> 00:49:42.090
And a language consists
of a syntax and semantics.

00:49:42.090 --> 00:49:44.570
So syntax is a
grammar that defines

00:49:44.570 --> 00:49:46.920
the form of the programs.

00:49:46.920 --> 00:49:49.730
And then semantics are
the rules that define

00:49:49.730 --> 00:49:51.540
the executions of programs.

00:49:51.540 --> 00:49:53.930
And then a compiler
is basically a set

00:49:53.930 --> 00:49:57.210
of rules that translate programs
from one language to another.

00:49:57.210 --> 00:49:59.990
And then verifications
is that you

00:49:59.990 --> 00:50:04.640
prove that the rules
preserve semantics.

00:50:04.640 --> 00:50:09.410
So here, we are going to talk
about a very basic expression

00:50:09.410 --> 00:50:14.360
language in which you are
allowed to do addition

00:50:14.360 --> 00:50:15.420
and multiplication.

00:50:15.420 --> 00:50:17.940
So this is just basic
arithmetic expressions.

00:50:17.940 --> 00:50:20.610
And the syntax that
you see over here,

00:50:20.610 --> 00:50:23.090
this is in a
language called Coq.

00:50:23.090 --> 00:50:27.780
And here it looks
a lot like Lean.

00:50:27.780 --> 00:50:33.480
It's similar, dependently typed,
functional programming language.

00:50:33.480 --> 00:50:35.240
And so, what you're
doing over here

00:50:35.240 --> 00:50:37.320
is that you're defining
this binary operator.

00:50:37.320 --> 00:50:39.170
This can be either
plus or times.

00:50:39.170 --> 00:50:41.630
You're defining this
notion of an expression.

00:50:41.630 --> 00:50:45.160
And this is saying that it
can be either a constant

00:50:45.160 --> 00:50:47.980
or a binary operation.

00:50:47.980 --> 00:50:50.440
And then, there
are some rules that

00:50:50.440 --> 00:50:57.260
tell you how to actually
evaluate these expressions.

00:50:57.260 --> 00:50:59.590
If you're given an
expression, what

00:50:59.590 --> 00:51:01.820
will be the result
when you evaluate it?

00:51:01.820 --> 00:51:04.360
And I'm not going to go
into the rules in detail,

00:51:04.360 --> 00:51:05.690
I'll share the slides.

00:51:05.690 --> 00:51:10.030
And if you are
interested, you can--

00:51:10.030 --> 00:51:14.120
well, you can look up
many resources on the web.

00:51:14.120 --> 00:51:20.650
But also, I'm happy to answer
questions over email offline

00:51:20.650 --> 00:51:22.930
or, actually, after this talk.

00:51:22.930 --> 00:51:25.580
OK, so then you define
the source language.

00:51:25.580 --> 00:51:28.660
So here, what you see is
that you can define the rules

00:51:28.660 --> 00:51:30.650
that we saw previously.

00:51:30.650 --> 00:51:34.540
They allow you to
define the semantics

00:51:34.540 --> 00:51:36.120
of quite complex expression.

00:51:36.120 --> 00:51:39.960
So here, what you're saying
is that you have a Const 2,

00:51:39.960 --> 00:51:44.540
so an expression can be a
constant or a binary operation

00:51:44.540 --> 00:51:46.170
or the result of a
binary operation.

00:51:46.170 --> 00:51:48.260
And you see that
this is the result

00:51:48.260 --> 00:51:51.890
of applying a binary
operation to two expressions.

00:51:51.890 --> 00:51:54.420
And so, this is a recursive
definition, really.

00:51:54.420 --> 00:51:59.090
And then you are getting
an expression as output.

00:51:59.090 --> 00:52:02.540
So here what you are
saying is that you

00:52:02.540 --> 00:52:08.120
can evaluate various complicated
expressions in this way.

00:52:08.120 --> 00:52:11.040
And so, now you have to define
the target language, right?

00:52:11.040 --> 00:52:12.080
So this is the--

00:52:12.080 --> 00:52:14.920
a compiler is a mapping from
a source language to a target

00:52:14.920 --> 00:52:15.420
language.

00:52:15.420 --> 00:52:17.370
So now you have to define
the target language.

00:52:17.370 --> 00:52:19.760
And so, what you
did over here is

00:52:19.760 --> 00:52:22.520
that you are defining
the target language

00:52:22.520 --> 00:52:25.470
as a list of instructions.

00:52:25.470 --> 00:52:27.930
So a program is a
list of instructions.

00:52:27.930 --> 00:52:30.350
And an instruction
is either this sort

00:52:30.350 --> 00:52:34.380
of a constant expression or a
binary operation expression.

00:52:34.380 --> 00:52:39.730
So one way to see it is that
while the original expression

00:52:39.730 --> 00:52:45.255
in the source language was
defined in this recursive way,

00:52:45.255 --> 00:52:50.180
in the target language, when
you are interpreting it,

00:52:50.180 --> 00:52:53.530
you are doing it using a stack.

00:52:53.530 --> 00:52:56.890
So this is a well-known idea
in programming languages

00:52:56.890 --> 00:53:00.580
that if you have a
recursive expression,

00:53:00.580 --> 00:53:03.170
let's say an
arithmetic expression,

00:53:03.170 --> 00:53:06.710
then the way you can execute
it is by maintaining a stack.

00:53:06.710 --> 00:53:09.460
And you can keep pushing
the current instruction

00:53:09.460 --> 00:53:10.220
on the stack.

00:53:10.220 --> 00:53:14.410
And you can basically do
this kind of a low-level--

00:53:14.410 --> 00:53:17.650
build up a low-level
machine, a stack machine.

00:53:17.650 --> 00:53:19.090
Actually, a lot
of early computers

00:53:19.090 --> 00:53:20.420
were based on this idea.

00:53:20.420 --> 00:53:24.950
And then the stack basically
keeps pushing new things on it.

00:53:24.950 --> 00:53:27.880
And then you pop,
and then you read

00:53:27.880 --> 00:53:30.610
the value that was just popped.

00:53:30.610 --> 00:53:36.650
So, while very basic,
it still captures

00:53:36.650 --> 00:53:40.100
a lot of important
programming language concepts.

00:53:40.100 --> 00:53:45.260
Now, the point is
that I want to believe

00:53:45.260 --> 00:53:48.650
that the mapping that I
would do between these two

00:53:48.650 --> 00:53:50.670
representations is
actually correct.

00:53:50.670 --> 00:53:54.930
But, of course, first I
have to define the compiler.

00:53:54.930 --> 00:53:56.610
And so, this is the compiler.

00:53:56.610 --> 00:53:58.910
So what this says
is that I am going

00:53:58.910 --> 00:54:02.727
to take my input expression
in the source language.

00:54:02.727 --> 00:54:04.560
And then I'm going to
do a pattern matching.

00:54:04.560 --> 00:54:07.250
I'm going to consider these
two cases, either when

00:54:07.250 --> 00:54:10.110
it's a constant or when
it's a binary operation.

00:54:10.110 --> 00:54:12.170
And in each of these
cases, I'm going

00:54:12.170 --> 00:54:16.170
to generate a target
language program.

00:54:16.170 --> 00:54:18.060
So in the target
language program,

00:54:18.060 --> 00:54:20.150
the entire sequence
of instructions

00:54:20.150 --> 00:54:22.100
is just written down as a list.

00:54:22.100 --> 00:54:24.538
Now, it's when you are
executing the program that you

00:54:24.538 --> 00:54:25.830
are maintaining a stack, right?

00:54:25.830 --> 00:54:29.630
That's when you are doing
all of the, kind of,

00:54:29.630 --> 00:54:31.450
modeling the recursion.

00:54:31.450 --> 00:54:36.520
But in the program itself, you
are just doing this kind of a--

00:54:36.520 --> 00:54:38.747
you are generating the
instructions from E2.

00:54:38.747 --> 00:54:40.580
You are generating the
instructions from E1,

00:54:40.580 --> 00:54:41.980
and then you are
sticking this b.

00:54:41.980 --> 00:54:45.160
And then you are just
building up a stack with--

00:54:45.160 --> 00:54:51.850
or a sequence of instructions
with all of this.

00:54:51.850 --> 00:54:54.970
So, for example, if you
started with this expression

00:54:54.970 --> 00:54:57.050
Binop plus Const 2 Const 3.

00:54:57.050 --> 00:55:00.860
So this is 2 plus 3 is,
that's really the expression.

00:55:00.860 --> 00:55:04.057
And so then, in the
lower level language,

00:55:04.057 --> 00:55:05.265
it's going to look like this.

00:55:07.780 --> 00:55:10.510
So then, you have to define
this correctness theorem, which

00:55:10.510 --> 00:55:14.690
says that for all e in the--

00:55:14.690 --> 00:55:17.080
all expressions e in
the source language,

00:55:17.080 --> 00:55:19.750
upon compiling this
expression, you

00:55:19.750 --> 00:55:22.000
get something that,
when you execute it

00:55:22.000 --> 00:55:24.140
in the programming
language's semantics,

00:55:24.140 --> 00:55:29.290
you get the same thing
as if you evaluated

00:55:29.290 --> 00:55:33.600
the original expression in the
source language's semantics.

00:55:33.600 --> 00:55:37.100
So these two are the same.

00:55:37.100 --> 00:55:42.020
Now, it turns out that COPRA
cannot prove this theorem just

00:55:42.020 --> 00:55:43.580
in one shot.

00:55:43.580 --> 00:55:48.960
So what you do instead
is that you ask the LLM.

00:55:48.960 --> 00:55:52.080
Again, think of the IMO
example that I showed,

00:55:52.080 --> 00:55:54.330
this is quite similar.

00:55:54.330 --> 00:55:58.700
So you ask the LLM to invent
a lemma, an intermediate goal.

00:55:58.700 --> 00:56:04.200
So the LLM goes ahead
and thinks for a while.

00:56:04.200 --> 00:56:05.670
And this is o1 here.

00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:08.930
And it comes up with
this intermediate lemma,

00:56:08.930 --> 00:56:12.110
which basically says
that, rather than trying

00:56:12.110 --> 00:56:18.890
to prove the entire compiler
correct, what you instead do

00:56:18.890 --> 00:56:24.270
is that you just show
one step of compilation,

00:56:24.270 --> 00:56:30.670
like for one subexpression,
that's going to be correct.

00:56:30.670 --> 00:56:34.280
So basically, that's
the target theorem.

00:56:34.280 --> 00:56:37.930
The auxiliary lemma is saying
that you can compile one

00:56:37.930 --> 00:56:39.860
instruction correctly first.

00:56:39.860 --> 00:56:43.940
And so this is really just
simplifying the problem,

00:56:43.940 --> 00:56:46.160
breaking it down in
a step-by-step way.

00:56:46.160 --> 00:56:50.770
And then we ask COPRA to come
up with a proof of this one

00:56:50.770 --> 00:56:51.800
instruction.

00:56:51.800 --> 00:56:55.400
And it's able to,
which is fantastic.

00:56:55.400 --> 00:56:58.450
But then, what we
can do is, just

00:56:58.450 --> 00:57:03.020
in the way we did previously,
we stick this back.

00:57:03.020 --> 00:57:08.120
We take this proof, give
it to the COPRA agent,

00:57:08.120 --> 00:57:09.580
put it in the
prompt, and then ask

00:57:09.580 --> 00:57:12.620
it to prove the entire
theorem correct.

00:57:12.620 --> 00:57:13.890
And it's now able to do this.

00:57:16.660 --> 00:57:23.150
So, that's what I have
on this particular topic.

00:57:23.150 --> 00:57:26.810
In summary, I would say that
mathematical discovery with LLM

00:57:26.810 --> 00:57:30.230
agents is extremely promising.

00:57:30.230 --> 00:57:35.420
I think that you can go a
very long distance with just

00:57:35.420 --> 00:57:38.330
in-context learning.

00:57:38.330 --> 00:57:40.850
But you want to have
the proof assistant

00:57:40.850 --> 00:57:42.960
feedback for proper grounding.

00:57:42.960 --> 00:57:46.610
And there is a lot of
higher-level agent design

00:57:46.610 --> 00:57:51.080
that you can do where you
can combine agents that

00:57:51.080 --> 00:57:54.560
are doing, for example,
formalization with agents that

00:57:54.560 --> 00:57:57.200
are doing hierarchical
planning with agents

00:57:57.200 --> 00:58:02.060
that are doing low-level stuff,
low-level proof generation.

00:58:02.060 --> 00:58:04.410
And this is a very new space.

00:58:04.410 --> 00:58:07.580
There is a lot to
be done over here.

00:58:07.580 --> 00:58:10.250
Now, one question,
again, going back

00:58:10.250 --> 00:58:12.380
to something we
asked previously.

00:58:12.380 --> 00:58:14.960
Can you get rid of
this interaction

00:58:14.960 --> 00:58:17.850
by just pushing the
whole thing to training?

00:58:17.850 --> 00:58:22.400
Maybe you generate a lot of
training data in this way

00:58:22.400 --> 00:58:24.460
by interaction with
the prover, and then

00:58:24.460 --> 00:58:29.960
you just continue training
the LLM with those tracers.

00:58:29.960 --> 00:58:33.100
And then at some point, the
LLM just gets good enough.

00:58:33.100 --> 00:58:35.200
We are not there yet.

00:58:35.200 --> 00:58:38.890
But maybe this is a world
that we can get to someday.

00:58:38.890 --> 00:58:42.760
But currently, we have
to have these sorts

00:58:42.760 --> 00:58:45.700
of agent architectures.

00:58:45.700 --> 00:58:51.230
Now there's this paper
several of us recently wrote.

00:58:51.230 --> 00:58:53.800
And you may have seen a
reference to this paper

00:58:53.800 --> 00:58:55.240
previously.

00:58:55.240 --> 00:58:58.930
Kaiyu is the lead
author of this,

00:58:58.930 --> 00:59:03.370
and Dawn was the leader
of the overall effort.

00:59:03.370 --> 00:59:06.080
But if you are curious
about this area,

00:59:06.080 --> 00:59:10.880
you want to know more about
specific challenges, roadmaps,

00:59:10.880 --> 00:59:14.160
then I would recommend that
you go and look at this paper.

00:59:17.230 --> 00:59:19.790
I had a few things to say
about open challenges,

00:59:19.790 --> 00:59:22.970
but I'm going to skip those
in the interest of time.

00:59:22.970 --> 00:59:27.960
And I'm going to move on
to the next topic, which

00:59:27.960 --> 00:59:36.300
is AI for scientific discovery,
because I want to emphasize

00:59:36.300 --> 00:59:40.680
that this idea of LLM agents,
this has a lot of value

00:59:40.680 --> 00:59:42.580
in this sort of
deductive mathematics,

00:59:42.580 --> 00:59:45.100
where you are starting
with a set of principles,

00:59:45.100 --> 00:59:47.760
a set of axioms,
definitions, and then you

00:59:47.760 --> 00:59:50.460
are proving statements
in a step-by-step way.

00:59:50.460 --> 00:59:52.500
But it also has
tremendous potential

00:59:52.500 --> 00:59:54.960
in empirical discovery,
which is what happens

00:59:54.960 --> 00:59:59.160
in most of the natural sciences,
where you are starting with data

00:59:59.160 --> 01:00:05.430
and then you are trying
to fit some hypotheses

01:00:05.430 --> 01:00:12.180
or some experimental claim, yes,
or some claims to this data.

01:00:12.180 --> 01:00:15.600
Now, we'll see that this is also
a place where the abstraction

01:00:15.600 --> 01:00:18.630
abilities of LLMs play
a potentially very

01:00:18.630 --> 01:00:21.010
interesting role.

01:00:21.010 --> 01:00:22.291
OK.

01:00:22.291 --> 01:00:27.550
So very quickly, this is
what a natural science

01:00:27.550 --> 01:00:28.790
process looks like.

01:00:28.790 --> 01:00:30.620
You choose a research question.

01:00:30.620 --> 01:00:32.270
You generate some hypotheses.

01:00:32.270 --> 01:00:36.910
You do an experimental
setup, you collect some data,

01:00:36.910 --> 01:00:39.950
you do some analysis of the
data, you interpret the data.

01:00:39.950 --> 01:00:41.900
And then you report
to the community.

01:00:44.540 --> 01:00:48.010
And so, just as an
example of this,

01:00:48.010 --> 01:00:53.090
if you think of the world of
astronomy, back in the day,

01:00:53.090 --> 01:00:57.830
there was this problem
that was observed,

01:00:57.830 --> 01:01:02.890
which is that people
believed that the Earth was

01:01:02.890 --> 01:01:04.610
the center of the solar system.

01:01:04.610 --> 01:01:09.170
But then some of the planets
appeared to move backward,

01:01:09.170 --> 01:01:12.220
and so this led to this
creation of this new hypothesis

01:01:12.220 --> 01:01:15.500
that, in fact, we have
a heliocentric model.

01:01:15.500 --> 01:01:17.630
And then this led
to the creation--

01:01:17.630 --> 01:01:19.770
the collection of more data.

01:01:19.770 --> 01:01:24.390
Tycho Brahe, in particular,
observed Mars and then came up

01:01:24.390 --> 01:01:27.660
with a whole bunch of precise
measurements of the planet's

01:01:27.660 --> 01:01:29.430
positions.

01:01:29.430 --> 01:01:34.050
And then, Kepler chose
to analyze this data

01:01:34.050 --> 01:01:37.540
and then fit a wide variety of
geometric shapes to this data.

01:01:37.540 --> 01:01:41.370
And then he came up with
this, Kepler's third law.

01:01:41.370 --> 01:01:47.460
And then, this eventually led
to Newton's law of gravitation.

01:01:47.460 --> 01:01:49.590
So there was this
process that happened

01:01:49.590 --> 01:01:55.480
in science, which involved
trading in abstractions.

01:01:55.480 --> 01:01:59.040
Seeing some data
and then coming up

01:01:59.040 --> 01:02:03.120
with this law in terms
of abstract principles,

01:02:03.120 --> 01:02:06.870
but then further generalizing
it into a law that

01:02:06.870 --> 01:02:10.530
was even more explanatory.

01:02:10.530 --> 01:02:15.120
So I think that LLM agents
have tremendous potential

01:02:15.120 --> 01:02:18.790
in automating a lot of
pieces of this pipeline.

01:02:18.790 --> 01:02:20.530
And in particular
here, we are going

01:02:20.530 --> 01:02:25.840
to focus on the problem of
symbolic regression, which

01:02:25.840 --> 01:02:30.460
is the task of coming
up with equations based

01:02:30.460 --> 01:02:33.280
on empirical observations.

01:02:33.280 --> 01:02:36.460
So, for example,
an instance of that

01:02:36.460 --> 01:02:39.820
would be that if you were given
these measurements of planet

01:02:39.820 --> 01:02:45.650
Mars and then you wanted
to discover Kepler's law,

01:02:45.650 --> 01:02:49.630
how do you do that
from the data?

01:02:49.630 --> 01:02:52.480
Now, one would think
that LLMs would not

01:02:52.480 --> 01:02:56.710
be a great fit for this problem
because the data is just

01:02:56.710 --> 01:02:57.740
really low level, right?

01:02:57.740 --> 01:03:01.030
This is just a bunch of numbers.

01:03:01.030 --> 01:03:06.940
However, it turns out that LLMs
do have a tremendous potential

01:03:06.940 --> 01:03:10.240
over here in the
sense that they can

01:03:10.240 --> 01:03:14.140
bring in prior scientific
knowledge into the picture.

01:03:14.140 --> 01:03:15.820
What you have to do,
though, is that you

01:03:15.820 --> 01:03:18.570
have to complement
the use of LLMs

01:03:18.570 --> 01:03:23.560
with the use of other machinery
that is able to, for example,

01:03:23.560 --> 01:03:30.000
evaluate hypotheses on the data
and give you some notion of how

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:32.460
well it performed.

01:03:32.460 --> 01:03:36.720
So once you have that kind
of a mechanism for evaluating

01:03:36.720 --> 01:03:40.590
LLM-generated hypotheses, you
can create an agentic loop

01:03:40.590 --> 01:03:44.620
where you have more and more
feedback going to the LLM.

01:03:44.620 --> 01:03:49.050
And then this process
of hypothesis generation

01:03:49.050 --> 01:03:51.120
is left to the LLM.

01:03:51.120 --> 01:03:57.160
Whereas hypothesis evaluation
is done by some other entity.

01:03:57.160 --> 01:03:59.130
And if you think of it,
it's not fundamentally

01:03:59.130 --> 01:04:00.870
different from the
way we use Lean

01:04:00.870 --> 01:04:04.040
or the proof assistant in the
previous part of the lecture.

01:04:07.320 --> 01:04:12.180
So one of the most
established approaches

01:04:12.180 --> 01:04:16.280
to symbolic regression
is genetic programming.

01:04:16.280 --> 01:04:20.800
And here are some visualizations
from a system called PySR, which

01:04:20.800 --> 01:04:23.540
is quite popular in this space.

01:04:23.540 --> 01:04:26.530
And so, what's going
on here is that you

01:04:26.530 --> 01:04:30.010
have a pool of candidate
expressions, candidate

01:04:30.010 --> 01:04:31.640
equations being maintained.

01:04:31.640 --> 01:04:36.400
And then you have mutation
and crossover operations

01:04:36.400 --> 01:04:37.910
being applied to those.

01:04:37.910 --> 01:04:41.950
And then, you are generating--

01:04:41.950 --> 01:04:45.700
you're updating this pool of
expressions using feedback

01:04:45.700 --> 01:04:49.630
from a separate machinery that
is evaluating these expressions

01:04:49.630 --> 01:04:52.520
and telling you, how
good is the loss?

01:04:52.520 --> 01:04:55.960
How well does this
match the reality

01:04:55.960 --> 01:04:57.105
that is there in the data?

01:04:59.620 --> 01:05:03.550
So this has had a lot of impact.

01:05:03.550 --> 01:05:08.780
There are applications in
physics and other areas as well.

01:05:08.780 --> 01:05:11.475
And so, here are
some example papers.

01:05:14.130 --> 01:05:18.660
So one thing that's
interesting is that if you just

01:05:18.660 --> 01:05:24.820
imagine how does this process
of genetic programming go on.

01:05:24.820 --> 01:05:28.050
So there is this
pool of expressions

01:05:28.050 --> 01:05:29.940
that is being evolved.

01:05:29.940 --> 01:05:32.010
And so, what you see
is that there are

01:05:32.010 --> 01:05:34.780
these islands of expressions.

01:05:34.780 --> 01:05:40.980
So these clusters which contain
expressions that are of--

01:05:40.980 --> 01:05:45.270
that have something in
common between them.

01:05:45.270 --> 01:05:50.880
So one way to think about
how LLMs are relevant

01:05:50.880 --> 01:05:55.260
is that these clusters or
islands of expressions, these

01:05:55.260 --> 01:05:58.600
capture various kinds of--
various classes of functions.

01:05:58.600 --> 01:06:01.150
So, for example, in
this red one over here,

01:06:01.150 --> 01:06:03.930
you may have
exponential functions.

01:06:03.930 --> 01:06:07.530
In this case, you may have
trigonometric functions

01:06:07.530 --> 01:06:09.960
where you have the cosines.

01:06:09.960 --> 01:06:13.090
And so one way to
imagine this is

01:06:13.090 --> 01:06:18.875
that an LLM looking at these
expressions, because LLMs

01:06:18.875 --> 01:06:20.500
are good at coding
and associating code

01:06:20.500 --> 01:06:22.360
with natural language,
could perhaps

01:06:22.360 --> 01:06:25.060
come up with some
explanation of what

01:06:25.060 --> 01:06:29.120
this piece of code, this
expression, is actually doing.

01:06:29.120 --> 01:06:31.240
And that's the
idea of abstraction

01:06:31.240 --> 01:06:33.550
that we are going to use.

01:06:33.550 --> 01:06:37.780
So this paper was
at NeurIPS last year

01:06:37.780 --> 01:06:40.870
and co-led by Aria
Grayeli, who was

01:06:40.870 --> 01:06:43.300
an undergrad at UT, and
Atharva Sehgal, who's

01:06:43.300 --> 01:06:46.600
a PhD student at UT.

01:06:46.600 --> 01:06:51.820
And so, the starting
point here is

01:06:51.820 --> 01:06:54.680
that we want to do this
kind of concept abstraction.

01:06:54.680 --> 01:06:58.450
And so, what we want
to be able to do

01:06:58.450 --> 01:07:03.640
is that we want to look at these
variety of laws, which come

01:07:03.640 --> 01:07:05.660
from very different contexts.

01:07:05.660 --> 01:07:08.770
But then we should be
able to look at this

01:07:08.770 --> 01:07:11.070
and kind of tell that
what's going on here

01:07:11.070 --> 01:07:16.530
is that you have this power
law thing going on, right?

01:07:16.530 --> 01:07:20.550
And so, the other
thing we want to do

01:07:20.550 --> 01:07:23.700
is that we want to use
background knowledge that

01:07:23.700 --> 01:07:29.070
was created by a physicist or
that's there inside an LLM.

01:07:29.070 --> 01:07:32.490
So, for example,
here, you have an LLM,

01:07:32.490 --> 01:07:35.110
a concept that
has been produced,

01:07:35.110 --> 01:07:37.260
which is that wave
strain diminishes

01:07:37.260 --> 01:07:38.530
as distance increases.

01:07:38.530 --> 01:07:43.500
This is, let's say, a
property of black holes.

01:07:43.500 --> 01:07:46.980
And so, you want to
be able to associate

01:07:46.980 --> 01:07:50.730
this kind of
information, this is

01:07:50.730 --> 01:07:54.900
a piece of common sense,
knowledge to equations.

01:07:54.900 --> 01:07:59.050
So this equation, for example,
satisfies these properties.

01:07:59.050 --> 01:08:03.780
But this is-- you want to create
a mapping between these two

01:08:03.780 --> 01:08:04.540
things.

01:08:04.540 --> 01:08:07.620
And because, if you imagine
how the scientific process is

01:08:07.620 --> 01:08:10.010
ongoing, so let's say you
have an AI that is predicting

01:08:10.010 --> 01:08:11.967
equations based
on data, you also

01:08:11.967 --> 01:08:13.550
want it to be
conditioned on this sort

01:08:13.550 --> 01:08:17.990
of high-level knowledge that is
being given by a human expert,

01:08:17.990 --> 01:08:18.858
right?

01:08:18.858 --> 01:08:23.520
And so, you want to have this
association between code,

01:08:23.520 --> 01:08:26.185
that is these
equations and language.

01:08:30.050 --> 01:08:37.189
So the way we set up this
symbolic regression problem

01:08:37.189 --> 01:08:44.810
using LLMs is something like
this, that we imagine that--

01:08:44.810 --> 01:08:47.130
and actually, here,
it's not just LLMs.

01:08:47.130 --> 01:08:49.250
It's more like a
combination of LLMs

01:08:49.250 --> 01:08:54.140
and this sort of abstraction
into high-level concepts.

01:08:54.140 --> 01:08:56.960
And so, we imagine that
there is this universe

01:08:56.960 --> 01:08:58.080
of high-level concepts.

01:08:58.080 --> 01:09:00.439
So, for example, wave
strain diminishes

01:09:00.439 --> 01:09:03.930
as distance increases or power
laws or sinusoidal functions.

01:09:03.930 --> 01:09:08.160
These are sort of high-level
ideas that we have in our head.

01:09:08.160 --> 01:09:12.000
And there is a notion of a data
set, which is the concrete data

01:09:12.000 --> 01:09:13.779
that you want to fit.

01:09:13.779 --> 01:09:16.660
And then you have a space
of programmatic hypotheses.

01:09:16.660 --> 01:09:21.779
This is, for example, the
landscape of equation--

01:09:21.779 --> 01:09:24.720
or the set of equations
expressible in Python

01:09:24.720 --> 01:09:29.580
or in some restricted
DSL inside Python.

01:09:29.580 --> 01:09:31.920
And then, what you're
saying is that you

01:09:31.920 --> 01:09:35.939
want to jointly
infer, given the data,

01:09:35.939 --> 01:09:39.899
this distribution over
pi, which is likely

01:09:39.899 --> 01:09:42.609
hypothesis and likely concepts.

01:09:42.609 --> 01:09:45.960
So you don't want to just
get likely programs, given

01:09:45.960 --> 01:09:47.819
the data, which
would be the goal

01:09:47.819 --> 01:09:49.660
of traditional
symbolic regression.

01:09:49.660 --> 01:09:53.160
You want to get both
the programs and also

01:09:53.160 --> 01:09:55.530
the kinds of concepts
that they represent.

01:09:55.530 --> 01:09:57.780
Because ultimately, you want
to explain these programs

01:09:57.780 --> 01:10:00.550
to a human scientist.

01:10:00.550 --> 01:10:03.300
Or alternatively, maybe
this joint distribution

01:10:03.300 --> 01:10:06.020
gives you more information
than if you just

01:10:06.020 --> 01:10:08.870
operated in the program space.

01:10:08.870 --> 01:10:11.760
And so, this you can
factorize in this way.

01:10:11.760 --> 01:10:17.360
And then it turns out that
the way to think about it

01:10:17.360 --> 01:10:20.750
is that p of D given
pi, is basically

01:10:20.750 --> 01:10:25.700
saying that if I have a
particular program pi, what

01:10:25.700 --> 01:10:28.980
is the likelihood of the data?

01:10:28.980 --> 01:10:31.970
So how well does the
program explain the data.

01:10:31.970 --> 01:10:34.910
In order to find this, you just
execute the program, right?

01:10:34.910 --> 01:10:37.380
And see what kinds
of results come out.

01:10:37.380 --> 01:10:40.820
And what the likelihood
of the data set is.

01:10:40.820 --> 01:10:45.860
And p of pi given C is
basically telling you

01:10:45.860 --> 01:10:47.620
that, OK, here are these--

01:10:50.297 --> 01:10:51.630
here is the concept that I have.

01:10:51.630 --> 01:10:54.240
So let's say I'm thinking
about power laws, OK?

01:10:54.240 --> 01:10:56.300
So conditioned on
this concept that I'm

01:10:56.300 --> 01:10:58.550
thinking about power laws,
what is the probability

01:10:58.550 --> 01:11:02.690
of this program that this
piece of code, right?

01:11:02.690 --> 01:11:06.990
To what extent does this piece
of code represent a power law?

01:11:06.990 --> 01:11:10.290
And this is something that an
LLM has background knowledge

01:11:10.290 --> 01:11:11.500
inside it to do.

01:11:11.500 --> 01:11:14.370
And then, finally,
I have this p of C,

01:11:14.370 --> 01:11:16.810
which is a prior over concepts.

01:11:16.810 --> 01:11:18.690
And this is also
something that an LLM

01:11:18.690 --> 01:11:22.110
knows that I have seen the
literature of, let's say,

01:11:22.110 --> 01:11:23.890
physics until this point.

01:11:23.890 --> 01:11:29.224
So what is the likelihood
of this concept?

01:11:29.224 --> 01:11:35.550
So all of this together
gives you, ultimately,

01:11:35.550 --> 01:11:42.270
the ability to infer both a
distribution over both programs

01:11:42.270 --> 01:11:44.430
and concepts.

01:11:44.430 --> 01:11:47.540
Now, in order to
really implement this--

01:11:47.540 --> 01:11:51.150
this is the loop
that we came up with.

01:11:51.150 --> 01:11:53.250
So this follows
the basic structure

01:11:53.250 --> 01:11:55.030
of an evolutionary algorithm.

01:11:55.030 --> 01:11:59.550
But it does this sort of
Bayesian concept learning thing

01:11:59.550 --> 01:12:03.040
guided by LLMs on top of that.

01:12:03.040 --> 01:12:05.830
And so, operationally,
the way to think about it

01:12:05.830 --> 01:12:09.530
is that you have, like in
any evolutionary algorithm,

01:12:09.530 --> 01:12:13.240
you're maintaining a population
of hypotheses or programs.

01:12:13.240 --> 01:12:16.060
And then, at this
point, you have a choice

01:12:16.060 --> 01:12:19.900
of doing evolution,
either guided

01:12:19.900 --> 01:12:22.180
by the classical
symbolic method where

01:12:22.180 --> 01:12:24.490
you're doing some random
tweaks to the abstract syntax

01:12:24.490 --> 01:12:25.820
tree of the program.

01:12:25.820 --> 01:12:29.230
Or alternatively, you are doing
this LLM-guided evolution, where

01:12:29.230 --> 01:12:31.400
you are saying that
here is a concept,

01:12:31.400 --> 01:12:33.340
here is a program
that I have, and I

01:12:33.340 --> 01:12:35.240
want to generate a new program.

01:12:35.240 --> 01:12:36.910
And when you are
doing that, you are

01:12:36.910 --> 01:12:39.940
being guided by this concept
library, which tells you

01:12:39.940 --> 01:12:41.710
that, these are the
concepts that I'm

01:12:41.710 --> 01:12:42.770
thinking about right now.

01:12:42.770 --> 01:12:46.750
So power laws seem really
good, really well-suited

01:12:46.750 --> 01:12:51.190
to this condition-- this
concept or this setting.

01:12:51.190 --> 01:12:55.750
Or maybe trigonometric
functions seem

01:12:55.750 --> 01:12:57.980
especially well-suited to
this setting, and so on.

01:12:57.980 --> 01:13:01.140
So you are feeding all of that
to this LLM-guided evolution

01:13:01.140 --> 01:13:04.680
process, and that allows
you to basically mutate

01:13:04.680 --> 01:13:11.700
the current programs
into new programs.

01:13:11.700 --> 01:13:13.995
And then, after you
do that, you are, like

01:13:13.995 --> 01:13:15.890
in the usual
evolutionary way, you

01:13:15.890 --> 01:13:21.540
are identifying the best
candidates in this population.

01:13:21.540 --> 01:13:24.240
And so, this is where you use
the fitness function, which

01:13:24.240 --> 01:13:32.550
is how well does your hypothesis
pool explain the data that you

01:13:32.550 --> 01:13:33.160
have?

01:13:33.160 --> 01:13:39.283
And this is where that p of
D given pi that comes in.

01:13:39.283 --> 01:13:40.950
And so, this is a
little bit more detail

01:13:40.950 --> 01:13:44.260
about how the hypothesis
evolution works.

01:13:44.260 --> 01:13:48.120
So you start with this
initialization, initial program

01:13:48.120 --> 01:13:49.840
population, and then
you're evaluating.

01:13:49.840 --> 01:13:51.310
You're getting the best program.

01:13:51.310 --> 01:13:53.560
You're mutating
or crossing over.

01:13:53.560 --> 01:13:59.250
And then you're replacing
the oldest program.

01:13:59.250 --> 01:14:01.945
And then this process just
carries on for a while.

01:14:04.640 --> 01:14:06.200
So the important
thing over here is

01:14:06.200 --> 01:14:09.500
that you are using the LLM
for implementing this mutation

01:14:09.500 --> 01:14:11.040
and crossover operations.

01:14:11.040 --> 01:14:15.422
So, in particular,
rather than in

01:14:15.422 --> 01:14:17.630
a classical genetic algorithm,
what's going to happen

01:14:17.630 --> 01:14:19.255
is that you're given
the AST and you're

01:14:19.255 --> 01:14:21.560
going to say that, OK,
swap out this subtree

01:14:21.560 --> 01:14:22.800
by a different subtree.

01:14:22.800 --> 01:14:26.270
Now you're going to say that I'm
going to ask the LLM to rewrite

01:14:26.270 --> 01:14:27.980
the program into something.

01:14:27.980 --> 01:14:33.110
And this idea was also
used in the FunSearch paper

01:14:33.110 --> 01:14:35.820
from DeepMind last year.

01:14:35.820 --> 01:14:41.010
And FunSearch has
been very successful.

01:14:41.010 --> 01:14:44.990
The way in which this LaSR
work differs from FunSearch

01:14:44.990 --> 01:14:48.110
is fundamentally in its use
of this concept library.

01:14:48.110 --> 01:14:53.420
But a lot of the other
ideas are closely related.

01:14:53.420 --> 01:14:55.730
So this is just to
highlight the difference

01:14:55.730 --> 01:14:59.020
between the symbolic crossover
versus the LLM crossover.

01:14:59.020 --> 01:15:02.070
So in this case, the LLM is
basically doing all this.

01:15:02.070 --> 01:15:04.000
It's being given
a crossover prompt

01:15:04.000 --> 01:15:06.060
and then it's given some
formatting instructions,

01:15:06.060 --> 01:15:09.060
and then it's generating
the new expression.

01:15:09.060 --> 01:15:12.660
So after you do this,
there is the-- so

01:15:12.660 --> 01:15:14.110
you have evolved
your hypothesis.

01:15:14.110 --> 01:15:15.370
So now you have this new pool.

01:15:15.370 --> 01:15:18.340
And now you also evolve
your concept library.

01:15:18.340 --> 01:15:23.460
And so the concept library,
this evolution process

01:15:23.460 --> 01:15:24.460
has these two stages.

01:15:24.460 --> 01:15:26.790
So first, you take
your new programs

01:15:26.790 --> 01:15:29.710
that you have created,
your new program pool,

01:15:29.710 --> 01:15:32.250
and now you're asking
the LLM to abstract this

01:15:32.250 --> 01:15:35.890
into these natural language
high-level descriptions.

01:15:35.890 --> 01:15:38.310
So, for example, here,
the LLM is telling you

01:15:38.310 --> 01:15:41.610
that, Oh, this equation
represents exponential growth

01:15:41.610 --> 01:15:43.890
or decay.

01:15:43.890 --> 01:15:46.290
And then, the LLM--

01:15:46.290 --> 01:15:48.720
the concept evolution
process is just

01:15:48.720 --> 01:15:52.390
like the hypothesis or
program evolution process,

01:15:52.390 --> 01:15:55.670
except it now uses this--

01:15:55.670 --> 01:16:02.420
an LLM to basically combine,
mash up these different concepts

01:16:02.420 --> 01:16:03.360
into a new concept.

01:16:03.360 --> 01:16:06.990
So, for example, here, you see
that exponential growth decay,

01:16:06.990 --> 01:16:08.280
this is one concept.

01:16:08.280 --> 01:16:11.330
Another concept says
depends on temperature.

01:16:11.330 --> 01:16:14.150
And our LLM, because it
has all this knowledge

01:16:14.150 --> 01:16:16.830
about all sorts of concepts
relevant in physics,

01:16:16.830 --> 01:16:18.950
it comes up with
this idea that, oh,

01:16:18.950 --> 01:16:21.540
this should be a
Boltzmann distribution.

01:16:21.540 --> 01:16:23.460
This is a useful
concept to have.

01:16:23.460 --> 01:16:25.070
And then this is
going to influence

01:16:25.070 --> 01:16:28.400
the hypothesis evolution, which
is going to influence concept

01:16:28.400 --> 01:16:30.120
abstraction and evolution.

01:16:30.120 --> 01:16:33.410
And this loop will continue.

01:16:33.410 --> 01:16:39.260
So one way to visualize this is
that after the first phase, when

01:16:39.260 --> 01:16:41.160
you have done this
hypothesis evolution,

01:16:41.160 --> 01:16:44.840
you may have these islands of
different kinds of expressions.

01:16:44.840 --> 01:16:52.250
Now, in the concept generation
process, concept discovery

01:16:52.250 --> 01:16:56.490
process, you're going to
get these natural language

01:16:56.490 --> 01:16:59.050
descriptions of these
different islands.

01:16:59.050 --> 01:17:02.440
So this is sinusoidal trends,
this is power law trends,

01:17:02.440 --> 01:17:05.850
this is linear trends, this is
exponential trends, and so on.

01:17:05.850 --> 01:17:12.480
And then, what we
found is that the use

01:17:12.480 --> 01:17:14.910
of this sort of a
high-level concept library

01:17:14.910 --> 01:17:18.070
helped us in this equation
discovery process.

01:17:18.070 --> 01:17:22.740
So we evaluated this on this
task of equation discovery--

01:17:22.740 --> 01:17:25.830
or discovering Feynman
equations, just given

01:17:25.830 --> 01:17:30.330
the prompt that--

01:17:30.330 --> 01:17:31.980
so there was the
data, but then there

01:17:31.980 --> 01:17:35.310
was a prompt that told
you which chapter you

01:17:35.310 --> 01:17:37.140
were looking at, and so on.

01:17:37.140 --> 01:17:41.160
And so what we found
is that this approach

01:17:41.160 --> 01:17:45.240
proved more effective than a
version where you were just

01:17:45.240 --> 01:17:48.270
using, either pure
genetic programming or one

01:17:48.270 --> 01:17:52.860
where you were not using any
kind of concept learning.

01:17:52.860 --> 01:18:00.090
And so it turns out that even
with local language models, when

01:18:00.090 --> 01:18:02.940
we didn't use a
frontier model, we

01:18:02.940 --> 01:18:07.680
were still able to outperform
this genetic programming

01:18:07.680 --> 01:18:08.890
approach.

01:18:08.890 --> 01:18:13.440
And the overall
finding was that--

01:18:13.440 --> 01:18:15.520
and you can find more
details in the paper--

01:18:15.520 --> 01:18:18.150
is that the use of this
sort of high-level concepts

01:18:18.150 --> 01:18:22.110
generated by the LLM, it
accelerates discovery.

01:18:22.110 --> 01:18:29.190
Now, one appeal of this
sort of LLM-based approach

01:18:29.190 --> 01:18:34.270
is that it can very naturally
take in user-provided hints.

01:18:34.270 --> 01:18:41.610
So, in this case, we
gave the algorithm

01:18:41.610 --> 01:18:45.390
some hints, which was this
is the kind of equation you

01:18:45.390 --> 01:18:47.010
are looking at.

01:18:47.010 --> 01:18:51.290
So in the previous experiments
actually, we did not have hints.

01:18:51.290 --> 01:18:54.410
So this was just
purely based on data.

01:18:54.410 --> 01:18:56.620
But then, in this
case, we had hints,

01:18:56.620 --> 01:19:01.130
and then we found that hints
actually provided some benefits.

01:19:01.130 --> 01:19:04.310
So on the x-axis here, you
have the number of iterations.

01:19:04.310 --> 01:19:08.270
On the y-axis, you have the
fraction of equations solved.

01:19:08.270 --> 01:19:10.510
And then we found that
the use of the hints, It

01:19:10.510 --> 01:19:16.630
provided a modest benefit at
least earlier on in the process.

01:19:16.630 --> 01:19:21.100
So here is an example
of this in action.

01:19:21.100 --> 01:19:25.130
So here, this is the
classic Coulomb's law.

01:19:25.130 --> 01:19:29.720
And then PySR is this
genetic programming approach,

01:19:29.720 --> 01:19:37.090
and it gave this very unwieldy
kind of expression that,

01:19:37.090 --> 01:19:41.120
it turns out, is equivalent
to the ground truth expression

01:19:41.120 --> 01:19:44.758
but it required a lot of
simplification for you to-- it

01:19:44.758 --> 01:19:46.300
would require a lot
of simplification

01:19:46.300 --> 01:19:48.830
for you to go from here
to the ground truth.

01:19:48.830 --> 01:19:55.130
Whereas the LaSR approach
is able to basically--

01:19:55.130 --> 01:20:01.200
because of some of the knowledge
that is there in the LLM,

01:20:01.200 --> 01:20:04.250
it's able to reduce to
ground truth after four

01:20:04.250 --> 01:20:05.940
steps of simplification.

01:20:05.940 --> 01:20:08.540
Now you could say, wait a
second, isn't the LLM cheating?

01:20:08.540 --> 01:20:10.890
Because it already has
Coulomb's law in its mind.

01:20:10.890 --> 01:20:15.330
So how do you know that
this is not just leakage?

01:20:15.330 --> 01:20:19.020
And, of course, in this case,
there is that possibility.

01:20:19.020 --> 01:20:22.070
But that said, I would say
that the attraction here

01:20:22.070 --> 01:20:27.300
is that the input to the system
is just this data, right?

01:20:27.300 --> 01:20:30.560
It's just the
input-output values for--

01:20:30.560 --> 01:20:32.370
that would result
from Coulomb's law.

01:20:32.370 --> 01:20:34.800
And it's a very
low-level kind of input.

01:20:34.800 --> 01:20:38.420
The fact that it was able
to fit this to Coulomb's law

01:20:38.420 --> 01:20:40.890
is kind of interesting.

01:20:40.890 --> 01:20:45.470
But that said, we also did other
experiments on synthetic data

01:20:45.470 --> 01:20:49.590
where you just manufactured
a completely weird equation

01:20:49.590 --> 01:20:53.380
and a weird data set
coming from that equation,

01:20:53.380 --> 01:20:57.010
and then ask the system
to regenerate this.

01:20:57.010 --> 01:20:59.340
And we found that there were
substantial benefits even

01:20:59.340 --> 01:21:01.740
in that case.

01:21:01.740 --> 01:21:05.940
So here is some information
about some of the concepts

01:21:05.940 --> 01:21:09.160
that you ended up
getting from LaSR.

01:21:09.160 --> 01:21:13.320
So you see there is stuff like,
good mathematical expressions,

01:21:13.320 --> 01:21:15.040
exhibit symmetry or regularity.

01:21:15.040 --> 01:21:17.010
But that said, there were
also some expressions

01:21:17.010 --> 01:21:19.290
that were really--

01:21:19.290 --> 01:21:23.430
that didn't really
seem to mean anything.

01:21:23.430 --> 01:21:26.250
But at the same time,
there is no guarantee

01:21:26.250 --> 01:21:28.990
about the quality
of the concepts.

01:21:28.990 --> 01:21:30.850
This is all natural
language at this point.

01:21:30.850 --> 01:21:32.730
An interesting
possibility is to use

01:21:32.730 --> 01:21:34.830
some kind of a formal
language to describe

01:21:34.830 --> 01:21:37.980
the high-level concepts.

01:21:37.980 --> 01:21:41.800
Now we also use this in
a new discovery problem,

01:21:41.800 --> 01:21:46.320
because we were also worried
about the possibility of this

01:21:46.320 --> 01:21:49.860
being an artifact of leakage
in case of standard benchmarks

01:21:49.860 --> 01:21:52.000
like discovering
Feynman equations.

01:21:52.000 --> 01:21:57.240
And so, we used this method
to come up with a new scaling

01:21:57.240 --> 01:21:59.460
law for LLMs.

01:21:59.460 --> 01:22:06.070
And so, here, what you see is
the so-called chinchilla law.

01:22:06.070 --> 01:22:10.170
So this is a law that was
postulated in a DeepMind paper

01:22:10.170 --> 01:22:11.680
from a few years back.

01:22:11.680 --> 01:22:13.680
And so here, basically
what you're trying to do

01:22:13.680 --> 01:22:16.050
is to predict the
loss of the LLM

01:22:16.050 --> 01:22:20.490
based on these various
hyperparameters.

01:22:20.490 --> 01:22:22.690
And so, this was the law.

01:22:22.690 --> 01:22:25.125
So you postulate a scaling
law, you measure model loss

01:22:25.125 --> 01:22:27.000
with respect to
hyperparameters, and then you

01:22:27.000 --> 01:22:28.570
fit scaling law to data set.

01:22:28.570 --> 01:22:30.040
And in order to do
this, of course,

01:22:30.040 --> 01:22:31.415
you would need to
have a data set

01:22:31.415 --> 01:22:34.140
where you have a lot of values
of these hyperparameters

01:22:34.140 --> 01:22:39.930
and corresponding accuracy
values for the LLM.

01:22:39.930 --> 01:22:43.920
Now, we did the same
thing with LaSR.

01:22:43.920 --> 01:22:45.960
Rather than fitting
a hand-fitted law,

01:22:45.960 --> 01:22:48.550
which was done in
the Chinchilla paper.

01:22:48.550 --> 01:22:51.060
So they basically
postulated a law like this,

01:22:51.060 --> 01:22:53.830
and then they fit
the parameters.

01:22:53.830 --> 01:22:57.240
But by contrast, what
we did is that we just

01:22:57.240 --> 01:22:59.400
asked LaSR to go find it.

01:22:59.400 --> 01:23:05.790
And LaSR was able to come up
with this sort of a law, where

01:23:05.790 --> 01:23:08.940
we also had this parameter
called number of shots, which

01:23:08.940 --> 01:23:10.980
is that, if you are
doing prediction,

01:23:10.980 --> 01:23:13.380
what is the number of
examples that you're

01:23:13.380 --> 01:23:15.780
giving it in context.

01:23:15.780 --> 01:23:21.990
And this parameter is available
in some of these data sets.

01:23:21.990 --> 01:23:25.170
And so what we found is that
there was this interesting kind

01:23:25.170 --> 01:23:27.810
of law that came up.

01:23:27.810 --> 01:23:30.520
Now, this is just one example.

01:23:30.520 --> 01:23:33.310
It's not really very
comprehensive yet,

01:23:33.310 --> 01:23:35.400
but this just shows
that you can make

01:23:35.400 --> 01:23:39.400
these kinds of new
discoveries using this law.

01:23:39.400 --> 01:23:42.130
And so, basically, if
you interpret this,

01:23:42.130 --> 01:23:45.500
the way to think about it is
that a large number of shots

01:23:45.500 --> 01:23:48.480
gives poor results for
low capability models,

01:23:48.480 --> 01:23:52.010
but then, once the models pass
a capability threshold, then

01:23:52.010 --> 01:23:54.060
having more shots helps, right?

01:23:54.060 --> 01:23:56.870
And this is sort of a common
sense linguistic interpretation

01:23:56.870 --> 01:24:00.290
of the law that came out.

01:24:00.290 --> 01:24:07.140
So you can also do
something after this.

01:24:07.140 --> 01:24:08.750
So if you're thinking about--

01:24:08.750 --> 01:24:11.790
thinking of yourself as
an LLM scaling law person,

01:24:11.790 --> 01:24:14.750
you start with the Chinchilla
law from the previous paper,

01:24:14.750 --> 01:24:17.150
and then you say,
OK, maybe I should

01:24:17.150 --> 01:24:22.160
bring in this insight about
shots into the Chinchilla law.

01:24:22.160 --> 01:24:25.370
Combine the old chinchilla law
with this new discovery that

01:24:25.370 --> 01:24:29.690
was made by LaSR, and then
you find that this modified

01:24:29.690 --> 01:24:33.380
chinchilla actually
is able to do

01:24:33.380 --> 01:24:39.590
better than both the old
chinchilla and also the LaSR

01:24:39.590 --> 01:24:40.700
itself.

01:24:40.700 --> 01:24:44.340
And so, this is kind
of interesting that--

01:24:44.340 --> 01:24:47.837
and shows that this discovery
process using LLM agents--

01:24:47.837 --> 01:24:49.420
and this is very
much an agent, right?

01:24:49.420 --> 01:24:51.460
This is not just a one-shot LLM.

01:24:51.460 --> 01:24:55.560
You're using an LLM combined
with this various other tools.

01:24:55.560 --> 01:24:59.820
And so, what you are
getting out of this is

01:24:59.820 --> 01:25:02.880
can influence human
discovery as well

01:25:02.880 --> 01:25:04.500
and lead to something
that is greater

01:25:04.500 --> 01:25:07.110
than the sum of the parts.

01:25:07.110 --> 01:25:11.470
So in the interest of time, I'm
going to skip this next bit.

01:25:11.470 --> 01:25:15.330
But what I said there is
that these ideas can also

01:25:15.330 --> 01:25:17.280
be applied to settings
where you have

01:25:17.280 --> 01:25:20.580
visual high-dimensional
inputs, because now we have

01:25:20.580 --> 01:25:22.390
visual language models as well.

01:25:22.390 --> 01:25:26.010
So you can go crazy
and build these kinds

01:25:26.010 --> 01:25:29.790
of evolution-based agents,
where you have VLMS

01:25:29.790 --> 01:25:30.970
as part of the story.

01:25:30.970 --> 01:25:35.640
And I'm extremely optimistic
about this direction.

01:25:35.640 --> 01:25:38.520
So, in summary, I would say
that LLM-directed evolution

01:25:38.520 --> 01:25:40.620
is a powerful tool
for empirical science.

01:25:40.620 --> 01:25:45.300
And this point has already been
made previously by FunSearch,

01:25:45.300 --> 01:25:48.020
but now we see that we
can make it even more

01:25:48.020 --> 01:25:53.000
powerful with these
ideas of abstraction

01:25:53.000 --> 01:25:56.100
that LLMs can give you
basically for free.

01:25:56.100 --> 01:25:59.180
And even this can
be even extended

01:25:59.180 --> 01:26:04.100
to visual settings where you
have to use now visual language

01:26:04.100 --> 01:26:04.620
models.

01:26:04.620 --> 01:26:07.350
But hey, they're coming a long
way-- they have come a long way.

01:26:07.350 --> 01:26:10.820
And importantly, the
abstraction abilities of LLMs

01:26:10.820 --> 01:26:13.370
can also be really helpful here.

01:26:13.370 --> 01:26:18.050
Open challenges, verification
of hypothesis and concepts,

01:26:18.050 --> 01:26:22.190
concept representations that
go beyond natural language,

01:26:22.190 --> 01:26:25.530
scaling to larger search
spaces, input dimensions,

01:26:25.530 --> 01:26:28.190
and then even going beyond
hypothesis generation

01:26:28.190 --> 01:26:31.670
to other parts of the
scientific discovery process.

01:26:31.670 --> 01:26:37.920
But at the end, though,
I am very optimistic that

01:26:37.920 --> 01:26:41.610
LLM agents, not just LLMs, but
these kinds of agentic workflows

01:26:41.610 --> 01:26:45.750
where you have
combinations of LLM queries

01:26:45.750 --> 01:26:49.080
as well as other
kinds of machinery,

01:26:49.080 --> 01:26:53.140
they can be extremely powerful
in scientific discovery.

01:26:53.140 --> 01:26:57.330
They can help you come up with
new scientific hypotheses,

01:26:57.330 --> 01:27:00.520
and even do experiment design.

01:27:00.520 --> 01:27:03.490
And also, as we saw, in
mathematical discovery,

01:27:03.490 --> 01:27:05.230
they can be supremely powerful.

01:27:05.230 --> 01:27:08.700
And again, we are just
in the early stages

01:27:08.700 --> 01:27:12.900
of what I think this
research-- line of research

01:27:12.900 --> 01:27:15.330
will end up being.

01:27:15.330 --> 01:27:19.870
And this can have applications
in math, in science,

01:27:19.870 --> 01:27:23.250
in engineering, as you saw the
formal verification application,

01:27:23.250 --> 01:27:26.010
building rigorous systems.

01:27:26.010 --> 01:27:29.680
So the future is very bright.

01:27:29.680 --> 01:27:31.410
And with that, I'll end.

01:27:31.410 --> 01:27:35.850
I want to thank all my
collaborators and funders.

01:27:35.850 --> 01:27:38.690
And I'm happy to take questions.

